      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-02-08_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <head>WOMEN'S CHAT.</head>
        <p>WOMEN'S CHAT.</p>
        <p>The Mauaoleum at Frogtnore, in which the remains of our beloved Sovereign now rest, stands in the most beautiful portion of the grounds, not far from the tomb of the Duchess of Kent, the late Queers's mother. The builditsg, which is composed of polished aLd unpolished granite, is approached by a grand flight of steps, and over the fine portico is tbe following touceing inscrip- tion : " In this tomb, his sorrowing widow, Queen Victoria willed, that all teat "was mortal of Prince Albert should be deposited A 11, MCCCLXII, "Farewell, most rare one, here will I rest with thee, and rise again synh thee. in Christ." The interior is very gorgeous and in the style of the Medici tombs in Florence. The waile ;trotted are fac-d with the rarest marbleseand the many beautiful pictures include scenes from the Nativity,the Crucifixion and the Resurrection. In the middle of the polished floor is the Sarcophagus. This is of Her Majesty's favourite stone dark grey Aberdeen granite, and is supported by four kneeling angelic figures. On one side of the tomb is an effigy in white marble of the Prince Consort •in Field Marshal's uniform, and the robes of the Garter. On the other side will shortly be placed a statue of our Queen, Victoria the Beloved, which long since she inspected,and approved of. Considerable anxiety is felt by mambo's of the Royal Family, with regard to the health of Princess Henry of Beeeeeperg. who, though bravely endeavouring to bear up, is at times almost prostrate with grief. Her Royal Highness has never, at any period of her life been separated from her late Majesty,and for years past every moment of her time has been occupied in endeavouring to lighten the very heavy load resting upon our Sovereign. As soon as possible, Princess Henry will go abroad, first to her sister, the Empress Frederick, and thsn on a long round of quiet family visits. The four children of Princess Henry of Battenberg havernevee, too, known any other home than that of their grandmother the Queen, and will miss her sorely, Princess Ena, Princess Henry's only daughter, is a charming girl, and was a great favourite with Her Majesty. The Priucess, it will be remembered, was the first member of the Royal Family horn in Scetland,that event taking place a little more than thirteen years ago. Princess Henry's three sons are all handsome, well-grown teddies and dev,oted to their mother. Sandringham has hitherto been the favourite residence of King Edward and Queen Alexassdra. Of the Royal Palaces, Buckingham Palace and Windsor Castle are Crown property, and therefore come into possession of the King, but His Majesty never had mucn liking for Windsor, and it is thought that he will still prefer Sand. riegham. Balmoral and Osborne both belonged to Queen Victoria, to dispose of as she pleased. The maintenance of all these Royal residenc• s means a great deal to the various districts, and the local tradespeople will be anxious to learn whether or in what manner they are to be kept up in the future. In London the only suitab'e Royal residence for the King is Buckingham Palece. Our late Queen disliked the place, and for very many years never spent more than a few nights under the roof during the twelve months. Immense sums have been spent upon it, the ball-room alone costing upwards sf a quarter of a million of money, but the whole place is badly arranged, and it is said that the best thing the country could do with it would be to pull it down, and build a new Palace which would be more worthy of the Empire's capital. Of all Court pageants that of the Coronation Ceremony is the most splendid, and it is said tio take the greater part of a year to arrange all the details and rehearsals The coronation of George IV. ce st no leis than £240,000, but William IV. was more economical, and cut down his consolation expenses to about £30,000. The coronation is, of course, only a ceremonial in ratification of an event already of legal force, and may take place at any time. That of Queen Victoria took place on the 28:h June, 1838, a little over a year after her succession. In the Case at William IV. the interval .was two or three months longer, and in that of George IV, it was eigh- teen months. From these precedents it would not seem likely that the Coronation of King Edward Vii will happen until the summer of next year. Is would hardly be possible for it to take place this coming summer, and for several reasons this is the season of the year which would be preferred for such an event. Few comparatively realise bow much we owe to Queen Alexandra in the matter of the good taste,. end better styles in dress, that have prevailed of late years. Her Majesty has the most perfect taste, and she it is who has taught the people what to wear, and how to t wear it. An exaggeration he Queen has never ,con- templated for a moment. . Those fashions approved by her are mcdified and altered as her taste dictatee, but many modes there are, that find no favour in her sight. For deep mourning Queen Alexandra has a great distaste, and so soon as etiquette permits she will doubtless discontinue it. When the Duke of Clarence died, nine years age, her Majesty, it will be remembered, quickly discarded the heavy crape garments deemed necessary for conirt !morning, donning, in their place, the simple, soft, untrimmed, black toilettes she so greatly, preferred. Mauy women may be heard lamenting juat now, the fact that black unrelieved, is most unbecoming to them. In the majority of cases however this need not be. Good taste is quite as necessary when sable attire is in question, as a galaxy of colours. IA se, chiffon,net,and tulle, have a wonderfully softening effect, and one of these fabrics ehould invariably be employed for the neck band, and the how, or rosette that finiehes it. For dainty blouses,accordion-pleated black chiffon ism great request, also crepe de chine, and mousseline de Eoie. All 'these fabrics are particularly becoming, and the little touches of jet, which are permissible, brighten and lighten them delightfully. Given the fitted and boned linings—obtainable in all sizes at every large drapery house—the most limited kraowledge of the art of gown- building, is all that is necessary for turning out blouses of this description. Dainty little scarves for the neck are in preparation far early spring out-door wear. Tie se are sufficiently long to pass tw,c, round this neck,and make a good bow. White lisse mbroidered in black appears to be the favouri:e material, but they will also be shown in black and white Brusbels net,tulle,gauze, lawn, and areophone. Such scarves are easy to manutacture at home, the ende especially affording considerebte scope for the artis lc among us. The rational cycling costume has become such R Com- mon mode of attire in Paris, that everyone thought that it bad bccon3o the regular thing in France, for women cyclists. Yet it seems the law is against it, and the Mayors in some of the rural districts have issued notices to the effect that " persons of the feminine sex appeal- ing in meeceline disguise of any sort " are liable to be taken to t-,e lock-up. Legal opinion has' been sought upon the question, and it is declared that the action of the local authorities is in accordance with the law of the land. Possibly some of the Mayors have an eye to busi- ness in the biting out of skirts, after the manner of the verger of Rouen Cathedral. That gentleman Came tQ the conclusion that it was not seemly to admit women in bloomer costames but he thoughtfully provided a supply cf skirts, and charges a franc for the use of one for the time being. As the hi euriful cathedral is a great place of attraction, it is thc ught be must be rapidly amassing a tcztute.</p>
        <p>"111.1ADGZ:'</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-02-08_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>RUNCORN.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>NEGLECTFUL PARENI S AT</p>
        <p>Messrs F J Norman, W Timmins, and W Handley, were present at the hearing of two very shocking cases of child neglect, at the Runcorn Sessions, on Monday. The first was of a very distressing nature : John and Catherine Rourke, of Northwich, Widnes and Runcorn, were summoned in respect of their several children, Catherine 14; Annie 9, John 6, Margaret 2; and a baby which died at the age of 6 weeks was mentioned,— Inspectors Owen (Northwich), and Hackett (Warring- ton), of the N.S.P.C.C., and Sergeant Gregory and other Police Officers gave evidence.—The solicitor acting for the Society was Mr Knowles,Widnea.—Frocn the evidence submitted it appeared thlt the Rourke s had been under the observation of the Society for the past five years, and every ffort had been made to induce the parents to show proper and parental care to their offspring. The husband had earned for a considerable time an average of 323. 4d. a week, but even then he had so conducted his house that the N.S.P.C.C., the Sanitary Inspector, and finally the Urban District Council were obliged to take actine against him in Northwich. They were turned from house to house, and everywhere they settled 00 made their house abomiaable.—The special date, for which they were proceeded against on this occasion, wae the 31st of January. On that date the state of the house was described as "not fit for a pig-stye." and to find words in which to record the filthiness of the children would be impossible. They were badly clothed and" alive," said one officer. Another officer said "John had oe a shirt which was 9.5 ' black as his boots,' and when he visited the house, about a fortnight later the same shirt Was doing service. The Superintendent of Police &amp;so told the Bench of the children's state. This testimony was "the children were alive with vermin,"• end " he had never seen chit' dren in such a condition in E, n his life." Of furniture there was little or none ; there was an old mattress and a bedstead, but note vestige of bed-clothing —lnspeetor. Owen testified to the parties having been Drosrcuted,and on each occasion the huebaod had got off, although 1° was his firm belief that the male was the worst offender' —The Bench,who looked upon the case as a very serione one, committed both John and Catherine Rourke to prison for two months with bard labour, and told rheni plainly at the end of that time they would coma out together, and had better make a fresh start, la the meantime, the children would be taken care of In the Workhouse. Rourke knew very well that he could earn from 30s to 40s a week,.and with that sum he COllla easily get into a different position if he liked. The other case was that of Michael and Ann Corcor an' whom it will be remembered were a fortnight ago before he Bench, charged with the neglect of th air two child- ren, on the 12th of January and other dates. The female was also charged with allowing her daughter to beg, h' that charge was withdrawn.—The case a fortnight was fully gone into, and defendants were given a to. • night's grace es the male defendant alleged he could ge: no work—Evidence was given that during the Inalr fortnight the fCoroorans, who lived in Norman's-yard' had made na effort "to put their house in order." at both the male and female were seen to go and get liefr from several public houses. Michael Corcoran was 3 seen in a drunken condition by Sergeants Brewster 3 Gregory.—Corcoi an`s defence was that he did not sreP his money in drink and only drank what was given nh He had only been employed for a short time and ` _ _ d neer</p>
        <p>but</p>
        <p>o ag.</p>
        <p>and s idCl , 310 1. spori•• Inni; ' bad thOV</p>
        <p>spent 23 lid for a new bed.—Tho Bench considered f ,</p>
        <p>for</p>
        <p>opportunity bad been given him and his wife, and mitted both to prison for two months with hard lal</p>
        <p>a Picture</p>
        <p>is the snowy-white linen on the table, the laces and frills</p>
        <p>on the damsel, or the spotless collar and cuffs on the man.</p>
        <p>And there's more in the iron- ing than in the washing— more in the starch than in the iron.</p>
        <p>If Colman's Starch is used, the starching part of the business is all right.</p>
        <p>I I</p>
