      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-02-08_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <p>WARRINGTON TOWN COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>Numerous Increases of Salaries. Toe Meyer (Alderman otesrts) presided at the Monthly meeting of the Warrington Town Council, held on Tuesday evening. Tone were also present :—Aider- menFairel melt, Sutton, Heaton, rancomb, Hutchinson Seam Green, Menke Couneillois John Evans, S Davies, et. Jolley, T B Mdkin, Rogers, William Roberts. John Roberts, Baled, James Evans, Brougb. Smethurst, A J. Tinnion, Wilkinson, Pierpeint, Greening, Ellison, l'lumnten, Simpson,Peeco :k, Shillcock, Hulse, Bennett, with Messrs .1 Lyon Whittle (town clerk), 9 Wilson Peputy town clerk) T Longdin (borough surveyor), J airhurst (bor mei treasurer), J Dees (sanitary engin- eer), W S Haddock (rag engineer), W H Gramedale (elec- trical ,ngineer). 0 Madeley (librarian), J Sutcliffe (deputy surveyer). Saturday's Ceremonies Compliment to Townspeople. The MAYOR said that before proceeding with the or- dinary business of the Council he would like to -bear public testimony first of all. to the excellent arrangements which were made for the irhportant functions of Satur- day, and sscondly to the fact that those arrangements were made exceedingly easy of being corned out by the orderly, and he was going to say reverent, demeanour of the large crowds which filled the streets, and in front of the Town Hall. He had heard it said that a crowd of Warrington people was as orderly a crowd as was to be found anywhere. He was able to bear witaess to the fact. His own experience fully bore out the opinion he had heard expressed by others. It afforded him very great pleasure to publ:cly recognise these two somewhat important facts. Letter from Mr. Pierpoint, MP. The MAYOR said that since the special meeting of the Council was bell he had received the following letter from their respected member, Mr Piespoint :—House of Comntons, January 221.1, 1901. My dear Me Mayor,— lii great sorrow which is weighing so heavily upon all of Us I am impelled by my feelings to write some Poor lamentation t.) you, being convinced that your grief at the death of our beloved Queen will be so keen Obit yen will not think my letter oat of place. The lose ot our Qleon Is to each one of us a personal grief. Our !place is when we think how thankful we ought to be mr a beneficent reign of well over sixty years. One thinks with sadness now of what ene has read about, the girl-Q.:eels having auidenly to take upon her young Shoulders the burden of sovereignty. One thinks of her as one has seen her—the happy wife Rue mother, the grief-stricken widow—of this Queen of ours, always faithful tuber duties in happiness and in sorrow, always anxiously working for the Rood of the many peoples e"er wtom she reigned, faithful unto death. It is some L'llaeleleon to us to see the sympathy which is offered LY the people of other countries, even of those where "ete has been of late much bitterness against We value very hi doly the presence of the krerraat roe at the bealaide of his dying grand- ! Mother, also the brotherly sorrow displayed by the Deople iof the United States. I think that it will interest you if I give you an extract from a book (probably for- gotten) written by an American j mrnalist all but 42 years ago. In hie description of the opening of Parlia- ment be her Majtsty in February. 1859, he wrote • " The Qusen read it (her Speech) in a very deliberate and distinct voice, and, as She eespears says, ' with good emphasis and discretion.' Not a word was lost, and wiem she had finiined she appeared to me as majes- , float aa before she began. Her voica was pleasant, and her intonation sheweel that she understood and meant What she uttered. But it Rounded odd to beer that little !Hunan talk so supremely of her power. her authority` °ler time. her Navy, tier ministers, her neople, Ste., ess„ and yet there was a touch of the • moral suhlime ' in the dramatic situation, in the palpable evidences of the sur- r^nnding• divinity which cloth hedge a Queen,'" The writer would scarcely think in 1859 that it weuei not be till the first year of the 2011 century that this great and good Q leen of a vast Empire and of the hearts of her People would he taken by death. Even now we cannot but fool that " she shou'd have died hereafter." I trust that rnv letter will not weary you. I think that I know Your feelings ton well for me to have any such fear. We here are taking teooth of allegiance to King Edward VII., whom may God preserve.—l ans, my dear Mr. Mayor, ynura very faithfully, ROHRRT PINRPOINT. TO his Worship the Marini. of W.rrincton. Naughty Boys. Complaints by Councillor Smethu-st. The minutes of the Watch Catenates were of a formal character. The MAYOR praposecl their adoption. Alderman FIRATON ',Tended. Councillor SielerMease drew attentina to what he &amp;s- -cribed ae the ShatTlef,il behaviour of 30 or 40 boys whom thee afeTneou in Bewsey-street„ he had seen pelting mill girls with snow and cinders. The mAyoa said the proper mettind of drawing -attention to the matter would be to aj,",,m,., Intimation privately, and net as a umiltnAnt Upnn the minutes. ouncillor SMETIMIST.--1 hope you will take notiol of , it. The MAYOR eaye the necessary promise, and the Minutes were carried. Finance Committee. Alderman SHAW GREEN, in propocing the adoption nf hid minutes, said the Council would nntice that they eisd secured a fair amerint of money in order to meet the considerable capital exeenditure at present being lectured by the borough, and he was Pleased to sac they were able to do it on most reasonable terms. (Hear, hear.) The DrPorY MAYOR seems led. and the minutes were APp-nved. Compulsory Powers of Purchase for the Wheat Sheaf Inn. , At a meeting of the S,r.et Improvement and Light- ing Committee the Town Clerk reported thee the owner Of the two cettatres adjoining the Wheat Sheaf Inn required £B9O lot the same, when it was resolved that the property be included in the next application to Parliament for compulsory powers of purchase. The minutes were approved, on the motion of Alder- Men SUTTON, aen-wled hr Conneill 'r BENNWIT. Health Committee.</p>
