      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-02-08_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <p>,</p>
        <p>Paving and Sewage Committee:</p>
        <p>The minute 3 were of a lengthy bul formal character, including estimates for the paving of new streets. Alderman BEATON propoied _their confirinati 3n.</p>
        <p>Councillor TINNION seconded. Councillor MALIN asked whether any action had been taken as a result of the discassion on the minutes of the committee at the last ineFting. Eif)said that a large amount of maley was to be spmt in the making of these streets fie wishel to know whether the pipes had been laid. Alderman EINATON Bald the sewers and gas mains were laid before the making of the streeti was carried out. The minutes were then approved.</p>
        <p>School Attendance Committee. Increase of the Inspector's -_-,alarv.</p>
        <p>At a meeting held on thel7co January it was reaolved that the salary of the Chief Int/3 ,etor (Nlr. William Jonee) be ineremed by £5 per annum until a maximum of £l5O b 3 reached.</p>
        <p>Alderman Snaw GREEN, in moving the adoption of the minutes, drew attention to the resomtnendation for an increase of the salary of Mr Jones. He hoped that when he had given them the circumstances under which the increase was suggested that they would cordially approve of it. Mr. Jones was appointed an r‘flber of the Corporation in 1380 so that he was in his 21st year as an officer of the Corparatioa. He had theta as &amp;Ad Mr Riley, and the two carried out the dirties of the Education Acts in the borough. There were not very many children on the books at that time. The work had only just practically begun. le 1895 Mr Riley re- tired, and Mr Jones was appointed chief inspector, Mr. Riley had a salary of £136 103. They did not give the new inspector that salary. They felt as they had con- siderable expense tiles, would try to ecenorniz et aid they gave Mr Jones a smaller salary than his predecessor had. Since then they tied given Mr Jones an increase, and his salary at the present time was £l3O, or even now £6 10s less than his predecessor received in 1895. They had seen from the minutes of the committee and from statements at the meetings that the work of the department had very considerably increased during the period that had elapsed since Mr Jones was appointed inspector. The number of children on the books at that time was 10,500; to-day it was 13,000 and they had had to in- crease the staff. inee Mr Jenes undertook the duties very considerable improvements had taken place in the method and manner of the work being done. (Hear, hear.) As an illustration he might state that Mr Jones suggested that he would like to see more of the fathers of the children, and for eoveral years he had attended at his office two evenines in the week in order that when the fathers had fln'shed their work they might See him in regard to c maplafets ceoecirfaing their children. That was not done before, and considerable benefit had been derived from his self-sacrifice in that direction. Then they had now certain tont meetings every week. The monthly meeting or fortnightly meeting was found insufficient. Therm- was therefore a weekly meetinz. The duplicate register, which meant a tremendous in- crease in the work, but by it they were able to trace every single child in the borough and know whether it was at school or not. All this increase of work was the result of the careful and constant exercise of Mr Jones in the effort to have the duties of his office better ful- filled. The Committee proposed that his maximum. salary should he £lso,and that his present salary should be increased £5 yearly for four years, beginning at the end of March. _ . .</p>
        <p>Councilbr Sarm,cocx seconded, and the minutes were approved.</p>
        <p>Rivers Committee.</p>
        <p>Abst action of Flood Water. At a meeting of this cmatnittee, a letter from the representative of the Corporation on the Upper Mersey Navigation Commission on the monthly proceedings of the Commissioners, was reed, as well es one from the Acting Conservator of the Merge?, respecting a proposed meeting with members of the Mersey D-icki and Barbour Board and others with re•rict to the abstraction of fl water from the tidal !portion of the river, to which the T iwn Clerk was instructed to state that the representa- tives of the Council would be prep-trod to attend. The minutes were confirmed, on the Motion of the Mayon, seconded by Alderman SUTTON. Chief Assistant Water Engineer Appointed.—</p>
        <p>Further Increases of Salary. At a me Aing of the Water Committee, it was resolved that Mr George Mitchell be appointed chief as.istant water engineer at the salary of £3 per week ; that the follow ing increases in the pay of the staff of this depart. meet be mad,' :—J Gray, trom 25s to 304 per week ; C Horobin, from 20f to 254 per week; E P Tusn. from lii to 14s per week : C thil3W, from 10s to 12r per week. The Town Clerk reported the result of negotia- tions with the agent for Brooke's trustees for the pu-- chase of land and u iderground tunnel at Croft, when it was resolved,that the term, now siihmittel by the Town Clerk. including the pAyiner,t of £lOO for land and £5OO for 688 lineal yards of uadergrou ad tunnelling,be agreed to. Alderman FILINCOMB, in proposing the confirmation of these minutes, s-tid that Mr Mitchell had been engaged In the water deparment for over two years, and his salary had been £2 2+ per week, with addirional pay- ment for overtime. They now proposed to appoint him on the permanent traff, give him £3 weekly and not pay him for overtime, Flo was told by the engineer that Mr Mitchell WWI A ~e.y useful servant, and that he was well worth the salary they were offering him. The Committee had gone carefully into the matter of the small advances, and were periectly it:Willed in asking for the sancion of th. Council to their propogalA. Councillor demi EVANS aecoudel, and the minutes were approved. Much ado about 2s. 6d.</p>
        <p>The Mayor•and Commercial Competition. Technical Schools Tender Accepted. At a meeting of the Museum Committeo a report Was prefeated by the Library Sub-aomrai'tee on the library 'lows and staff. when it was resolved that the lending library be closed dady at 830 p.m ...except On ThUradarS, when it soil tw Closed at I p.m., that the wage of 3 W Howarth be ralaed from £2 to £2 2, 81 a week, and that the lender of Mr C W Davenport Cu. the erection of the technical actioA at a coat ot £13,649 be accepted. Toe MAYOR moved the confirmation of the minuteg.. and allude to the tender of Mr Davenport for the erection of the Technical School. Be said he was not a member of the sub-committee who received and con- sidered the tenders, but they had repottcl to them as a Museum Committee by the sut-oommittee that it was the lowest tenaer, and it was recommended for accer- Canoe. In regard to the necessity for the confirmation of the tend-r that night. certain parts of the Old Barracks—the whole site of which was required for the new police built-line—had been .occupied tor some years by the Technical Ilstructioa Committee in con• nee ion with certain classes. It was impassible for the police bei'dmcs to. .1a c,,mplettd until the Technical , Instruction Committee vacated that portion of the Old I Barracks site. So far as the work of the Technical I Instruction Committee was c,mc .rned It had been ham- pered, harassed and hindered from the begi-ning by varied cireumstanct s. •0e of them was—and he was almost tired of referring to it—the atr-minable manner in which the committee's funds had been manipulated by the County Council 0! Lancasb,re to the irdvantagn nt other districts and to the disadvantage and CChit Of Warrington, The fact that there had ten ceptral building in which the varied ci,,sses could tr) carried on—the want of centralization —had been a very serious hindrance t.,4 the won( of vie C,emmittee. They wanted to imprdve all that and make the InJtitutioa ivcrthy of the town</p>
        <p>P</p>
        <p>land district and equal RN/ to the req direments of its I trade, Mr. Chamberlain en the one hand, and Lord Risebery on the other nand, had spok in receatly in con- I neczion with technical instrucl in to the effect that the I war of the nations of the future was to be a commercial war rather than any other kind of war, and, tneref ore. it bahoved England more tiara any other nation, in consequence of the fact that .he development abroad had been on such a rapid scale, to set tneir house in order, and that quickly, so that the youth of this nation should be fu'lv eqaipped to meet the competition that Was beco fling ()lily in ire serious Or the part 01 their Conti- nental rivals and American c 'ileitis also. Lie future of the c mutt.; so far as its c immerce was co,icerned depende ILIA large extent—and to a greater extent. than they could appreciate—upon the manner in which the youth of the country was educated, hot in the old- fashioned style, but that there should be something or the character he had tried to shadow forth in the pr' - pose's they work submit'ing that night with regard to the erectian of the building. (Hear, hear). Alderman SHAW GREEN seconded the confirmation of . the tninute, and alluded to the minute that the waged of JVI Howarth be raised to £2 2s 61 a week. Eztra duties, the Alderman explained, had been put on Mr Howarth owing to the removal of one of the staff to the Town Hall and the sub-conamitfee were of an opinion, that some slight increase ought to take place. Councillor A J JOLLEY contended that the a,nount i involved was small; but the principle at issue Was an I important one. He admitted that Mr Howarth WB9 an 1 I extremely capable official and worthier of a higher peal- ' tion, but the position he occupied Was a sunordinee one. Quite recently a string committee reported on the duties and ate remuneration of the whol 5 staff employed in the carious departments of the museum, and it fixed a maximum of £2 a week as sufficient for the duties devolving upon ale klowarth, and when once a maximum for any official, subordinate or otnerwise, had been fixed by a competent authority, it ought not to be varied without very strong reasons baiaig given. The method was not satisfactory, and to hind extremely distasteful. The resolution was only carried by a narrow majority at the committee, aud he moved as an amend- ment that the particular minute be not confirmed. Alderman MONKS seconded, aid said he did so practi- cally for the reasons which had been already given It did not seem quite conaiatent after a sub-committee had reported as to whet was in their opinion a maximum pay, that a proposal shoald come forth in a month practically to give an advance. The sub-committee had recommended that no advance should be eiven. This was a matter of principle, and the BUM of £2 was suffi- cient to pay a man in the position in which Mr Howarth was placed. He was worth more, but working as the committee was working, with a limited income, they could not afford to pay more. Councillor GREENING fiAid he sh;u'd also support the amendment on principle. Only a fortnight before the Library Sub-committee took tots matter in hand, they held an important meeting to consider the salaries of the officials. Alderman Shaw Green was preiient,and agreed with the committee. (Hear, hear, from the Alderman. and "At that time.") Then came the question of the re-organisation of the junior staff, and it was referred to the Library Sub-committee, and Alderman Shaw Green supported them. The members of the sub-committee had over-stepped the orders they had received ; they were to consider the remuneration of the juniors and not of the seniors as they had done. Half-a-crown a week ' was not a great.amount in itself, but the principle was a great one, and he objected to any sub-cernmittee attempting to take upon themselves a duty which had not been committed to them. . _ . . .</p>
        <p>Councillor WILLIAM ROBERTS would support the minute as it stood,frorn what be heard about Mr. flowarth from others. The subordinate officials of the Museum and Library were exceedingly attentive and obliging to those with whom they came into contact. It was all very well to say this was a matter of principle, but he con- tended that if it had been an advance of £lOO to a higher official they woull not have bad any objention at all. (Laughter.) That was the first time he had heard an amendment propesyl at a meeting of the Council on a qceetion of a principle involving an increase of 2. 61 a week. He contended that it was beneath the dignity cf I the Council to go on with the amendment, and he I should, therefore, take the side of the junior officials. The Mime said the committee bad to depend en- tirely upon what a three-half-penny rate brought in. There had been a tendency for so.° time for the establishment expenaes (salaries, gas, water, ike.) to in- crease out of proportion to the increase of the income. The establishment charges had absorbed a laraer pro- portion of the income than the purposes for which the building as a whole was intended to have absorbed. It was not bocause the members did not think Mr Howarth was not well worth the £2 2,6 I but because the payment of another 24 6d a week.small as it might appear to be, was part of a whole scheme in regard to which they bad to be extremely careful. He was extremely sorry for many reasons to be in opposition to Mr Howarth. A more courteous, painstaking officitl they had net, but it was the poverty-stricken condition of the purse at their dispOtial they had to consider, owing to the fact that they were not like any other committee of the Council. They could not increase their expenditure beyond their income, or apply for a supplementary vote. Alderman SHAW GREEN regretted the difficulty that I had arisen and contended that eel principle was involved. llt was a sub-committee of the chairmen of the various sub-ceratnittees of the institution which met to c -insider the general circumstances, and they agreed that as things were they could not reasonably increase any of the salaries. But between thed and the time thie r solution was carried a very important change took place that altered the whole condition of things ; and it was owing to that alteration that the proposal came before them. Mr Crank who was then in charge cf the technical in- struction department at the Museum, was removed to , the Town Clerk's department, and they were without anyone at the Museum to undertake that work. A sine- eestion was made by the curator and the secretary of the committee that Mr Howarth shoull undertake a certain proportion of that duty hitherto perforrne I by Mr Crank and Mr Parkinson another portion of duty ; and between them the work hitherto done by Mr Crank was done. Eger&amp; labour of a special kind was pat on Mr Howarth. They saved 30s a a eek by Mr Crank's re noval, and gave Mr Parkinson an increase of 6i per week. A lady assist- ant Was engaged at 12s a week, and they could therefore well afford 0 pay Mr Howard 21 61 a week more. They considered that a man who had extra work put upon him was entitled to extra pay. (Hear, hear.) Councillor RENNET! . sAd he thought the explanation of Councillor Shaw Green was satisfactory, and th it the amendment ought to be withdrawn. On a vote, the amendment was lost, and the minutes Were passed in their entirety. Markets Committee.</p>
        <p>Mr. Wallington's. Salary. It was resolved at a saes-log of the above committee on the 21st January, that the salary of Mr W. H. Wallington, the assistant-inspector of markets and weights and measures, be Increased from £2 to £2 2. per week. Alderman Hurcunesofe moved the confirmation of the minutes of the above committee, and alluded to the pro- posed increase of Mr Wallington's salary. Us pointed out that he had been with the Corporation about seven years, and the committee thought the time had arrived when his salary should be increased. The sum named was a small one considering the v duable sery c a he bad rendered. Mr Wallington was a very efficient st-e Ind officer. (Eiger, hear.) Councillor ROGERS seconded, Councillor James Everts moved that the minute the ' re -</p>
        <p>relating tO the increase be'referred back, and that the saltry he increased by ss. Mr Wallington was worthy of an Increase. He considered that to offer a man of Mr Wallington's abithy 2. a week increase was an insult. There was no seconder, and the minutes were agreed tn without alteration. Gas Officials' Salaries Increased by £7O A Year. At a meeting of he Gas Cummitsee held on January 18:h. it was reso!ved that the Engineer br. author's to attend the annual conference of the I,stitute of Gas Engineers, to be held in London, in February next, that the ,esohition of the Council of s:h January. 1897, confirming the report of ttw Special Committee atp aim el to consider the salaries of officials, be reseirlied so tar ' as it affects the maximum • alary of the GAS Engineer; tbat the salary of Mr W S Haddock be inereared from £4OO to £450 per annum, and that the salary of Mr S B Chan,lev, the Assistant Gas E igineer, 133 increased From £l5O to £l7O per annum. The Dirvrery Mayon p-opsed the confirm ation of the minutes. Speaking of the recornmecd ttion for increas- ing the salaries of Mr Hadd ck and Mr Chandley, he said the advances were quite deserved. SheA Mr Haddock's appointment Me output of wolk4 had</p>
