        <p>tth Gn Committee re tom.nended that P‘W"g Cth Collin for e erention of the new IP,rda R recording to the specifications arid F the atheeenr tender eir He of rr „a! em epenteriee prepared by Messrs. William and Se',3r Owen at £2,575 be accented. I Alderman FATECLOunn move(' and Alderman Hitercate- udmerwoeflß.4Wn ca innei n o do e nn(;,7, ctohnafit •L the SON seconded the adoption of the Commit, e minutes. acceptance Cooci l lo ro fT rtos e oppoqed the acceptance of this tender atthe Purpotes Committee's m .eting and he now intended to opp„, it again. He did not iutenl to deal with the moral and social aeFect of he question, but simply the financial standpoint The ere - y areoimen'; or 'Laser. tin which he had heard in favour of the prop met re- erectioa of the hotel was that they ahould drat with C n- °oration property as they would deal meth their own. ,It was said that the surrenderinv. of the licence r would mean a loss of £l,OOO or so, whieh wa3 con- sidered the value of the licence of the ot hotel. Ile ' would like to point out that the land and the new hotel, together with the furnishing of icovould cost the Corpor- atian £B.OOO. Was it advisable to risk that amount on the off-ehanco of getting something in addition fet the licence ? There was no trade in connection with the place, for the old hotel bed been pulled down,and it was proposed to re-erect it in a street which was already OW nrowdra with license I houses. He thought it was a mistake for the Corporation to embark on this txpenditure for he did not see tioevrth,y were go- ing to get their Money back. Personally he was a teetotaler, he did not insist that everyone shoal conform to hie p trticular views. He felt that the Cor- pora ion '" would nave this hotel left on their hetat, end it would do a ceesie, r able mount of iej iry to the -hop keepers if another licensed place were put in Beeler. street. He had noticed that "the trade" had abstained from voting when the matter had been brought forward. They had a good sprinkling of the trade on the C. uncir, and he would be glad if they would give tee members the benefit of their advice as to the proposal to establish a public-house in that neighbourhood. (Hear. heir.) Councillor LUKE Eeresom secsode4 toe emeedineet. The Council had been eccused„ he sail, of doing some ry wicked things from time to time, but in his opinioe they never did a more wicket thing Chau when they proposed to rebuild the Feathers Hotel. Councillor BENNETT said he was strongly in fay nit of the principle known as the municipalisation of puerile- houses, for he believed it to be a practical an 1 teasibit way of promoting temperances reform. If the reseution of the committee was a step in that direction he would be pleased to supp nt it. He woull like to point out that from the temperance point of view, there was no particular advantage to be gained by stirrendering the licence, for if a man wanted to get drunk Inc would go to anyo ee of the d ,zen isublic- houses in the same street. There Was not the slightest reason to believe that because there were eleven houses instead of twelve, a man would resist t drink, fie faded to see why this property etemld be flung away. He had been acewed of being quis)tic. hut he had never been Po q wixo'ic as to pro- pose that certain property which beiongrd to the Cor- poration, and winch they had every right to use in a proper way, should be thrown awsy. He toupee the Corporation would build the ho!-.el and afterwards manage it themselves, on lines winch had been tried with great tIr:CEBR elsewhere. He believed It was possible for the Corporation to faro's!) an object leisen on how te conduct a licensed hens,' and to materially oromete the interetr,of temperanc There was no reason to believe that it could nor 1 e made to pay. The hotel would not be a "tied house," and they would be in toe position or being +OA^ to buy absolutely the best of everything and this would tend to increase the custom and make it an object lesson to the aah)le trade. .The management of public-houtree on proper lines world hi favourable to temperaece. (Hear, heat.) Alderman SilosEs remarlvel that if the idea of the Conned Was to build this hotel and then eell it to sone - olio else ale should ohj sot to the foiling of i, nut if trie Council would held tele hotel sn I exeiriineet on the lines suggested by Councillor Bennett he 'i.heu.d le pl. ased to eurmert the propoaition. Be would like to a-k the Town Clerk whether the Ceuecil had the ueeeleere legal p.,w,n'a to spend money for suh 4 par;.. He as that euggesti dby Coencillor Bennett. le -hold Os rather surprised if the Town Clerk said they had. The MAYOR : ftl9 whole q a •Sti,) h-a-1 Oe-in ! on the suppesition that we are acting legally under thel advice nf qv. Town Clerk ' !</p>
