        <p>Councillor WILLIAM ROBERTS sopporced the amend- ment as A tradesman in Bridge-street. If the Cor- poration built this hotel opposite the Lion Hotel they would have 14 licensed homes' from Market Gate to 13:iitga Foot. Hi would ask them to follow the examp;e of the Loaders County rouocil which had extinguished no fe Ner than 47 Iliceased houses when they had come into their po phi) for insprovealeuts. This policy had been supo,,rted by all parties and carried by a very large majority. fh/re were certain public-houses off Bridge-street which were approached by pas laves, such as the Eagle arid Child, the Green Dragon, anS ' the George Inn. These would have to bi renew( d when Bridge-street was widened, aus if th,. Corporatioo carried out this proposal of' r boillmrug them, the result would be that teese licensed houses which now occupied a back position would be brought to the front ot BrioNe- itreet. He really thought the !suggestion of Alderman F's' c -nu," was more preferable , to the pre.ent one. If toe Council would not agree to the forfeiting of the licence, why not take the hotel to Springfield-street near the Parr Hall. (Cries of "Oh ! ! ") Cimacilior WILLIAM ROBERTS : r knew what a storm of opposition such a proposal would arouse. ihip•ople in that locality would resent it v,,d.y stroney, btu; it thii Corporation woofs' not put thi hotel there why put it in Bridge-street, where it was not wanteo ? There was i not a single parasol in Bridg-i-street who would not say " le: it go and wool go with it" Councillor B mnett was hh 'cued with the idea ni the manicipali-lation of , t,uolic-hous,s, but was there the slightest intentioo on the part ot the Council to carry out thisugge.tion 1 C.maai:hr TINNION :N. Cllll3Cillor WILLIAM ROBSRTS Said it Was important that they (Mous o know. He might 1: Wrong but he wa told there was ri it the slightest intent the part of 1 the Goancil to carry not any BUM) so`ierne. The idea was to seli the lintel tc• the highest 