        <p>carrying on a profl'able businssi in a house ofjthia kind to face of the competition of 11 or 13 °then several of which had a struggle for bare .xiatence ? it they could' . not carry on a financially successful bus ness without trespassing very closely indeed upon the limits of the law, were they justified as a Corporation in aading an- otter temptation to theoyerflevring facilities that Already existed ? He ventured to say they had not. They would not be doing justice to the p esition which they occupied as he representatives of the ratepay era if they allowed the mere miserable financial aspect to outweigh all other considerations. (Hear, hear.) To put the Council in the position of adding temptation§ to an air. &amp;4 orersup- pu 0 district and to run the risk of having to carry on the foulness contrary to the law of the land in order to make it a financial suceess, was a very serious porition far tbem to face. He would willingly support Councillor Be.nriett's views if he tnought there was a reasonable chance of the C operation heing allowed to conduct the hotel themselves, but be lud very grave doubts about their being allowed to do so. He did not know of any law which permitted a Corporation to traffic. in a trade of this kind. Alderman lisAvow There is no law to prevent us. C turicillor BENNETT was understood to say that Blackpool was already mAing the experiment. Tne MAYOI3 said that authority might have sufficient po .vers, bu: they as a Corporation had no power to 'anal:A) them to become the business proprietors an hotel except of the property itself. Fle would gladly support an experiment on the lines suggested, te.cau.se he was anxious to see it tried. If the Corpor..ti3n fulfil itself compelled to sell the hotel, ;kith, u :le he did not believe the [o.ition to be a good one, be eh mid prefer to see i; cold to a committee or company of the same type, as he thought Earl Grey and one or two nth: rA were coon..ct. d wkth, where the profits after the payment of five per cer-t. on the capital invested, were devoted to objects of. necessity and charity in the town in which these licences are granted. If the sale of this property had to be made as a foulness inat,er to somebody who would take the whole of the p.roft's. then he was afraid the last atavt went I be far worse than it was new. (he Cern,. a: ion had fl maay •an occasion acquired prcpertv which could have been re-sold at a profit, but t-.ey had not hesitated to let it go when it wus required for s public improvement. He koltrat upon the case of the, Feathers hotel as a (petition of public improvement. He did not see any reasonable objection to allowing the licencn to lapae. It was well knaven that the business done in two of the licensed houses in the neighbourhood Was even more wretched irk amount than rho busine..a in the cld Feathers hotel. He had said what ho had jamt uttered on the subject with al respect to the convictions of r tear gentlemen, and having at the same time a strong belief that every member would vote commis ntioualy either for or againat it according to the views lie held ou this particular ques- tion. (Bear, hear.) rote was then taken.</p>
