      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-02-08_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>LETTERPRESS PRINTING,</head>
        <p>LETTERPRESS PRINTING,</p>
        <p>PAPER RULING, AND BOOKBINDING, IN ALL THEIR BRANCHES.</p>
        <p>PROURAMMES rou BAZAARS. PROGRAMMES rou SALES,OF WORK.</p>
        <p>ON Thursday the young 1 Queen Wilhelmina's Wedding. Queen of HOLLAND was married to Duke Harm* OF MECKLENBURG. and everybody in this country will wish her much happiness in the union. The Queen of HomaND won all hearts by her natural unaffected sincerity and homeliness when;she was amongst us a few years ago. The late Queen VICTORIA was greatly attracted to her, and recog- nised in her a charming young woman, ;in whose future she felt a lively interest. If she has not lived to see the marriage, she was aware that it was fixed, and she approved of the choice, when it had been made, of a King CoNsoRT. The Queen of HOLLAND was assigned to many eligible Princes and Dukes before a definite engagement was announced, and even when she had set her heart on the present marriage she waited for some time before making the official announcement and con- senting to the indispensable preliminaries. There were also:negotiations with the Dutch Government as to the status of the King CONSORT, or Prince CONSORT, or whatever he is to be called. The Dutch people did not quite see that they were called upon to make the Queen's husband an allowance, and the Queen herself met the difficulty by proposing to assign to him a sufficient income for his position out of her own ample Civil List. Everything has now been satisfactorily adjusted, and the marriage took place on Thursday under the most happy auspices. The Hague has been en fete for several days, and correspondents who have arrived there from attending our Queen's funeral are much impressed by the joyous demon- strations which are taking place in the Dutch capital. The Dutth are not quite so phlegmatic as is ordinarily supposed. They can be grave tough on occasion, and probably these occasions are many, but they seem determined to be gay over the wedding of the young Queen, and they are entering upon the febtivities connected with it in the most thorough-going fashion. The town is adorned, the people are decorated,and the country is of:one mind. The young Queen begins her reign much as our Queen Victoria did, allowing for the differences of time and situation. People are always more generously disposed to a young Queen than they are to a young King. The difficulties of rule are recognised, and allowl.nce is made for the difference between a tender woman in dealing with affairs of State and a strong man addressing himself to the business of his life, Queen WILHELXINA has already endeared herself to her people by her frank, womanly ways, and we hope there is no danger that she will ever be immersed in political difficulties that cannot be overcome by the aid of her Ministers. In this country there was some surprise that she was so eager to welcome Mr. KRUGRR, but no stress was laid upon it. She simply yielded to the natural impulse of her heart, and when Mr. KRUGER presented himself at The Hague she conducted herself with a skill and pru- dence we were compelled to admire. A hint had been given that England was the friend of Holland, and that it would be unwise to impair this amity by any false step. The friendship between the two countries is of long standing. The relations between the two Royal Houses are inti- mate and cordial. This marriage should maintain everything in its undisturbed condition. We wish the young Queen complete domestic happiness and a long and brilliant reign. She has many excellent qualities, a fresh heart, a bright intelligence, and an independent will. The country itself is undivi- ded and prosperous. The Prince Consort will do nothing to embarass the Queen, to interfere with her Ministerd, or to render himself unpopular with her Ministers, or to render himself unpopular with the nation. He is of German lineage, but he has to become Dutch, and we believe that he has the</p>
        <p>and the tact to fill a most important role with- out disparagemant to his own connections or injury to his Queen-wife and her people.</p>
        <p>PARLIAMENT IX to have "Of Great Public business of great public Importance," importance brought before it immediately it assembles. Mr BALFOUR SO de- clares, and he ought to know. The public can only guess at its nature, and perhaps the guesses may not be altogether inexact. If there had been</p>
        <p>no sorrow such as the QUEEN'S death has caused we should have connected the business of the opening with the South African War. The necessities of the, situation, however, compel attention to a new settlement of the Civil List, and we take that to be Mr BALFOUR'S meaning. The KING is in a peculiar position. The income he had as Prince of Wales passes to the Duke of CORNWALL and of YORK. The settlement the country made respecting his children comes to an end. The arrangement made with the Crown at the beginning of the last reign terminates. A new settlement has now to be established, and it , is,the one urgent business of Parliament, before which everything else must give way. The KING will come to Buckingham Palace to receive the Prime Minister and to approve th 3 Speech, which he himself intends to read from the Throne. Parliament is to be opened in semi-state, not in full state, and as soon as the Address is voted the next business will be the settlement of the Civil List and the apportionment of an independent income to the Queen CONSORT. Various rumours have been current as to a considerable increase in the Civil List. It may be just as well to believe none of them. We must await the declarations of the Government and the appointment of the Select Committee to whom matters of this delicacy are usually remitted. Ample provision will be made, we do not doubt, for the KING and the Queen CONSORT. There is no need for either niggardliness or extravagance. The country can afford to do its duty in a just and generous manner, and no political interests _will be served by endeavouring to make party capital out of rigid economy on the one hand, or out of abound- ing munificence on the• other. The KING] is a sensible Monarch, and he will desire to disturb as little as possible. He has shown his desire t 3 walk in the late QUEEN'S footsteps in everything, and therefore we do not think he will make exacting demands, or wish to assume the title of British Emperor so many fulsome flatterer would like to bestow upon him. He is Emperor of India, of course, and there are local native Princes who submit to his sway; but in the length and breadth of the rest of the Empire there is neither ruling Prince nor King. The expedi- ency of including the Colonies in the KING'S title is a matter for separate argument, and we shall be wise to decline any change if it means that we are to comprise everything in the title of Emperor; though even in that case some definition would be needed. We believe that EDWARD VII. will be sincerely desirous of accepting the Crown,the Con- stitutiou, and the Civil List as they have come down to him, and that this view is consistent with everything he has said and written since be came to the Crown. Until the contrary is shown it will be well, in our judgment, to regard as un- wise the several attempts to create the impression that a good many things are going to be changed because we have a King instead of a Queen. The less change there is the better. This may be old Whig doctrine, but it is common sense. We have not consented to a revolution because we have mourned over a dead Queen.</p>
        <p>Electricity is a comparatively new force, and we are, the majority of us, only imperfectly ac- quainted with it. The uses it can be put to are fairly familiar, but its dangers are much more of a mystery, and disasters such as that which occurred at Liverpool on Monday bring home to us with startling force the fact that the increasing utiliza- tion of electricity adds very materially to the perils which surround us in everyday life. Two men and two horses were killed, and a number of ' people severely injured by an accident simple in II itself but made horrible by the electrical compli- cations. A number of the telephone and tele- graph wires which run in all directions over Liver- pool broke owing to the weight of snow upon them and fell into the street. There was nothing very alarming in such an occurrence itself, but the wires unfortunately came in contact with the heavily charged cables carrying the electric current 1 for the trams. The hitherto harmless wires then t became "live" and flew violently about the street, white-hot, and almost instantaneously killing two men and two horses who came in close con- tact with them. Indescribable terror and confusion followed, and the horrors of the situation were aggravated by the temporary powerlessness of the onlookers to help those un- fortunate beings who were r3ceiving the terrible electric shocks. Eventually the suffering and the dead were removed from the fatal coils by means of planks, ropes, and other non-conductive agencies. Such a dreadful fatality as this has seri- ously alarmed dwellers in towns and cities where electric traction is used. Proper precautions against such an occurrence will be imperatively , necessary in the many places where they are non- existent, and in Warrington it behoves our electricians to provide that such an awful catastrophe will be impossible when the electric tram service is instituted.</p>
        <p>The marriage of Queen Wilhelmina is raising in Holland a number of delicate questions of etiquette, such as were experienced in Great Britain when Queen Victoria accepted Prince Albert as her consort. It seems rather strange that in the daily drives of the engaged couple the lady should bow profusely while the gentleman sits stolidly indifferent, as it were, to the compliments being offered, and without the courtesy to acknow- ledge the honour paid to his fiancée. For him to respond, however, wonld mean laying himself open to the charge that he accepted the tribute as intended in part at least for him, and that would be very bad taste. In the funeral pageant in Lon- don on Saturday, in the same way, King Edward was unable to make any recognition of the raised hats of the people, because the action was a mark of respect to the dead Sovereign ; but Queen Alexandra returned the salute, because her position in the line made the demonstration in her cue unequivocal. Another point of Royal eti- quette, while perfectly reasonable, has since been commented upon in connection with the ceremony in the Solent. Alone of all the croft on the water the yacht which bore the Queen's eldest son bore its flag aloft without any sign of monrning. This of course, was because the King's standard can never be lowered in his own territory. But the comments on this apparent omission are said to have been nearly as frequent as the mistakes made by so called musical people in regard to the marches played by the massed bands in the procession. stop- ition</p>
        <p>News comes from Russia of the forcible page of the "Northern Courier,lthe one Opposit paper which remained in the Czar's immense inions. This journal WaS iounded about a year ago by Prince Bariatinsky. iii3lllo of the most eminent Russians in the world of learning and science had contributed to its columns, and just before Christmas it celebrated its first anniversary with much rejoicing. However, it had made the fatal mistake of exposing the present social, economic and moral conditions of Russia ; and shortly after that festival its career was brought to a close by a midnight visit from the Chief In- spector of Printing Offices—illegally, it is said, for he had only received two warnings as to the tendency of its policy, instead of till ee, which it seems te have had the right to expect Under the Censorship law. Yet even the Liberal Russian monthly," Vestnik Terropy,".in summing up, as so many journals in all countries have been doing, the progress of the past century, mentioned, no doubt with a due sense of what was required by prudence, the great measure of freedom of speech and opinion now enjoyed throughout the empire. The same writer, as quoted in the " Anglo- Russian," ha.s the audacity to add: "The uevt</p>
        <p>I</p>
        <p>dom-</p>
        <p>(1.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-02-08_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
        <p>Governor-General has displayed not only groa forbearance but also a truly humane considera- tion and a most obliging attitude with regard to the newspaper press of Finland; during the past two years only seven papers have been totally sup- Pressed,and about twenty more stopped for longer or shorter periods !" As a matter of fact, eleven have been suppressed and thirty-one suspended. ut what a world of meaning in that "only I" Or is the writer, under the mere pretence of subser- viency, indulging a splendid irony?</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-02-08_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <head>MATTERS OF MOMENT.</head>
        <p>MATTERS OF MOMENT.</p>
        <p>A Provincial Letter.</p>
        <p>The lot of the new King can scarcely have been °tie to be envied hitherto His days have been full of business and worry,and even his nights 'lave not been restful. It passes into a proverb ten he was Prince of Wales, that he was one of 13 busiest men in England. He had then nothing .P7t hatever to do with affairs of State,but was mginly 4"l!onsible for the discharge of those multifarious oUties which constitute the social and so—called il)arnental side of Royalty, and for which the ritish public are almost unsparing in their de- As Prince of Wales, he was a social tac- t'clan of the first rank, and has always been re- ruarkable for his ability to say the right thing at the right moment. Nor have his increasing years served to abate anything of his personal activity,as has been made abundantly clear during the past three weeks. Yet, with one exception, Edward VII. is much older in acceding to the throne than was any of his predecessors.</p>
        <p>From an authoritative source, says a newspaper correspo dent, comes the information that her 2lajesty has left a million of money to be partly dis- posed of by the bequest of both Balmoral and Osborne to King Edward Vl.,and that a large sum of money in which the late Queen had a life interest u.nder the will of the Prince Consort goes by rever- sion to the young Duke of Saxe-Coburg and Gotha.</p>
        <p>Writing of money matters, it may be noted that there are at present five judges on the, Bench Who might re;ire with pensions of £3,500 a year each if they cared to do es, but they do not. They are all competent to sit, and by doing so they save tbe nati m £17,500 a year. But, such is the selfish- flees of human nature, the Bar object to this, for the reason that there are others anxiously awaiting PrOmotion, and if these five judges would only elect Ro into private life, it would mean five vacancies .er five hungry waiters for the judicial loaves and uabes.</p>
        <p>Failing the introduction of corridor carriages for 910 protection of passengers, it is suggested that Intercommunication should be secured by removing tie upper portion of partitions between carriages. Ste is nothing new in the suggestion. The plan -43 be,9l:C in operation on the Brighton and the aion Underground railways some years. For Unexplained reason the average railway pas- tw-g5l seems to object to intercommunication be- d; eon carciages, besides which, he points out the „:""vantage that only the first and last compart- -entB of the series contain alarm bells as .a rule.</p>
        <p>b verVbody in business nowadays is supposed to • e "on" the telephone. It is interesting to note, therifore, that in the provinces the first Corpora- tion to inaugurate its own system of telephonic 'communication has been that of Tunbridge Wells. The municipal system will extend over the district for ten miles in all directions. Already a large number of subscribers to the National Telephone have seceded, and are subscribing to the municipal I,lndertaking which will cost them £3 or .£4 a year itzs, while the profits will go to diminish the rates e Whole scheme, when finished, will have cost 7.?_thething like t; ten thousand pounds. Other 'urPoratiens "pie ase copy."</p>
        <p>bnA new source of revenue for seaside resorts has 44-en " discovered "by Great Yarmouth, where ptches "for minstrel entertainments along the Sea front have been let at certain rentals. Other seaside resorts should be on the quivive in this Matter. Considerable harvests are reaped by itiner- ant musicians in this way, and they could very 'Well afford to pay the cost of policing and regulat- ing portions of the beach they have been accus- lemed to occupy without paying any rent at all. A arge income is waiting to be picked up by the authorities, as every pound gathered in would be Profit, the expenses being about nominal. Benefits, Moreover, other than pecuniary would accrue. The lesQees would be under control; misconduct would Mean loss of the licence ; and abetter class of enter- tainment would be ensured. Anyhow, it is clear that local chancellors of the exchequer have hit 11Pln an Eldorado, since Yarmouth contemplates receiving £5.35 a year for the two rings that are to be permitted on,the beach.</p>
        <p>.It is perhaps not generally known that not a mingle case of rabies was reported to the Board of ttgr.iculture last year from the whole of England. supposed that Mr. Walter Long's. reason for !ving to quit the representation of Liverpool for that of Bristol had something to do with his dog- puzzling policy. If so, the facts would appear to llPtifY the policy rather than Mr. Long's late con- atituency.</p>
        <p>--- It will surprise no one to hear that never has the -18aring: 3f mourning apparel been so general as the death of the Queen—for the very simple that never before has any similar event Itilealed so strongly to people of all ranks in life.' the provinces travelled a long way to London Telegraph mentions a case in which ladies ti order to he furnished with ready-made dresses of ablek material, Provincial tradespeople will prob- d.'Y not be slow to take the hint. It is not only ti`eases and mantles, however, but also black gloves, 489 etc., which have been in great demand, and in o.dition to material for garments, large quantities •„1. ,black drapery were sold for buildings, draping ,onpits, the churches, chapels and so forth. An ex- Pt predicts that after the term of half-mourning OrPires, there will be a strong reaction in favour of I'„dant colours ; which is possible, since sombre colours have a depressed effect, and makes one Winaetiraes wish that ladies wore white, instead of rje,k, as a sign of mourning. Assuredly Fashion vllig,nt make an exception in the case of young ban'ciren, but even with adults, white would be N're Suggestive of Christian hope than of the gan ideas of death. But in a time of mourning, °f course. regard has to be paid to what is cue- Lott_ • t,e_ary,and for a time at lesst,advocates of Funeral -uk form have to remain silent.</p>
        <p>DHLICIOUS DIAZAWATTIBB 111 A MILICIOUS MAZAWATTBS TB DHLIOLOUS tdAZ tWATrila TB.s. aez.z Aomirrs: 1111501 X isiumsa • co., LTD. WBOLEEALTS AND RETAIL GROCERS 5. RosairacAsmar-enurarr, WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>' ty7A,7., .11% "</p>
        <p>In every town and village of the United King- dom, as well as in the Metropolis, on Saturday the bells expressed in solemn cadence or in impressive tfo.notone, the sorrows of a people united in their grief. and the majestic service in memory of ,the deceased Queen Victoria was said in thousands of Places of worship. A hush fell upon the haunts of commerce, and the instinct of a race that is not emotional in its grief, and is not unmarked by a ' Sense of the dignity of restraint, pointed to making the day on which the body of the much-loved Sovereign was borne to the !spot she herself chose for her funeral, a day of outward expression of a aYmpathy and respect such as had never been accorded before to any ruler the world had known. There was on that day new meaning to the familiar Words " The mourners go about the streets." We 'were all mourners, and there was none so destitute of love for his land and of respect for his Sovereign as .not to feel tho influence of the day, and not to Join in spirit in the ceremonial which fittingly a:larked the sense of the nation's loss in the removal one who had been our chief representative as 4otig as most of us can remember. This was one of those times when "The trappings and the suits of Woe" are not without meaning, but are the fit expression of a nation's sorrow. The funeral obsequies of Saturday last formed an appropriate tad to a reign which was as brilliant as it was 'beneficent.</p>
        <p>. Para VETICRINLRY Boo.—we have received a :Pecinaen copy of tho Veterinary Book, which is being 7,:ent out free of charge by the Proprietors of Candy's ruid Veterinary Remedy, which may be obtained on taplication to Mr H J Condy, of 65, Goswell-road. Lon tio don, The book is really a most useful little publica- . n, and contaics instructions from veterinary surgeons the highest standing for the treatment and cure of t• lenfs and oomplsints affecting horses, dogs, cats, Lie, sheep, pigs rabbits, poultry, pigeons, cage birds, t °ld Ash, ite.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-02-08_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
        <head>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>SUPPER AT THE MERSEY Missrox.</p>
        <p>The Bible class conducted by Mr W Shaw (lay reader) at the Mersey Mission to Seamen, Runcorn, on Wednesday evening, sat down to a well served supper, and spent a pleasant evening under the presidency of their leader, in the schoolroom attached to the mission. SUDDEN DEATH AT WIDNES. On Saturday Patrick Partland, aged 57 years, 27, Cresant-street, died after a short illness at 12.15 a.m. Deceased was a healthy man all his life up to a week ago, when he complained of having a cold. He stayed away from his work on the 28th instant but refused to have a doctor. He gradually became worse and died without having been attended by one. The facts have been reported to the coroner but no inquiry will be held. RE-OPENING OF HOPE HALL, RuNconN. Many of our readers will learn with pleasure of the re-opening of Rope Hall, Bridge-street, as a Gospel Mission. The venture was made on Sunday last and was attended satisfactory results. In the morning the service was conducted by Mr A Jones (missioner) and there was a fair congregation In the afternoon a Sunday school was formed there being nearly 100 persons present. At the evening service Mr Spiby, of Widnes, conducted and preached eloquently, to a crowded congregation. Solos were sung bp Mr Miller "Showers of Bless- ings"; Mies Greest, 'The walk of God " ; and a duet by Messrs Jones and Harrison "Will your anchor hold." The large congregation joined heartily in the refrains. On Monday night there was a meeting which successfully terminated in the re-opening of the Band of Hope. The chair was occupied by Mr Charles Beswick, who in his address spoke very encouragingly to the young people, and urged them to take up the cause of temperance. He was pleased to take the chair in a building which had been a disgrace to the town. For, though that place was originally des- tined for a House of God,it had lattErly been known as a place of "jugglery and boxing," and every- thing that tended to demoralise our young people. Mr. Beswick also referred to the good times he had in past days spent in the Hall, when it was a power for good, and hoped that every effort would be put forth to once more make it worthy of the name "House of God." He felt sure that everyone would do all that lay in their power to assist Mr Jones in the good work.iSolos were sung by Miss M Greest, and the following recitations were given :—Miss Worrell," We are Seven " ; L E Rowlands," Don't Quarrel"; Estha Turner, "Two Mice "•, SI E Worrell, " Out in the Street"; Miss Copeland," My father was a drunkard" ; and Miss Grecs t, " Bonnie's Prayer."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-02-08_WidnesExaminer_art0041" n="art0041">
        <head>RUNCORN NOTES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN NOTES.</p>
        <p>[BY A CORRESPONDENT.] The Nonconformist Simultaneous Mission will be commenced in St. Paul's Church, Runcorn, before I again write, therefore I take this opportunity of urging all Nonconformists, whatevar their influ- ence may be to join early in the good work and carry it through to a successful issue. Runcorn is' a field ripe for the mission worker, for its people are good at heart though perhaps neglectful. Look at the state of crime as presented from year to year in our Police Court, and it is easily discernible that the townspeople figure very sparsely as offenders; more than one half the indictments of any serious- ness being usually laid to the charge of the floating population. This is to our credit. Though to our discredit stands the fact that not one-sixth of our male population attend a place of Divine Service with anything like respectable regularity. The problem for the Mission to solve is how to get us to church and keep us there. We are approachable, but decidedly apathetic, that is all ; and, if the Mission drives away but a half of the apathy, it will have accomplished a blessed work.</p>
        <p>The signs of the times are healthy too, for this week we record the cleaning, renovating, and re- opening of Hope Hall, Bridge-street. Mr A Jones is the energetic Missioner entrusted with the management, and a good start was made by him on Sunday last. I trust this may meet the eye of some enthusiastic young Christians, and be the means of bringing him assistants in his work. The reproach that the once flourishing Gospel Hall had degenera- ted into a " penny gaff " will then be heard no more, and our town will be brighter and healthier for the change.</p>
        <p>Tho lectures and socials at the Liberal Club have for the last few weeks been disarranged owinz to the National mourning consequent on the death of our late beloved Queen. but I understand when re- sumed they will go on to the end of March smoothly enough. Much good work has been accomplished, and the members of the club generally have an ap- preciable keenness for political history which is truly encouraging to those who Dave the arrange- ment of the lectures in hand. The meetings will henceforward be open to ladies ; it is therefore hoped that many will avail themselves of the opportunity afforded them to shed their influence socially and acquire some additional political education.</p>
        <p>To those who wish to object, it may be interest- ing to learn that the Local Government Boird in- quiry, respecting the application of the Runcorn Urban District Council to borrow .£lO,OOO for the purpose of the Widnes and Runcorn Bridge Act, will be held at the Town Hall, Runcorn, on Thurs- day next. So far, all has worked well, and the horizon has been clear of the grumbler, but there is no telling what the inquiry may bring forth. Not many points of objection can be raised, for the investment of a" certain sum" has been provided for by Parliament. The question of" poverty" might be raised, but to those most acquainted with the needs of the district, it is so apparent that the Bridge will pay, that that objection vanishes before the resplendent fignres put forward to illustrate the profits.</p>
        <p>Apropos of the Bridge question, there is a rumour afloat in what may be termed well informed quar- ters that the L. and N.-W. Railway Company will not be sorry when the Transporter Bridge is an accomplished fact. It is well known that the Com- pany intend widening their line to four sets of rails from Holyhead to Chester, and it is now rumoured that it has been the intention of the Company to eventually double their line from Liverpool to Ches- ter as well. The Runcorn Bridge hitherto has affor- ded a problem only to be overcome by tremendous expense, but the advent of the Transporter Bridge has opened up the possible consideration of the Closing of the footbridge, at present in use, and the ultimate widening of the line, if not to four, to three rails; which with loops at either extremity of the short distance, will, it is expected, prove as efficacious as four. Let me repeat, it is but rumoured, and I give it with all reservation.</p>
        <p>"Honour to whom honour is due." The whole of our townspeople will no doubt be delighted to learn that the Runcorn Urban District Council, has so far honoured our respected townsman Mr W Handley, as to accept, and hang on the walls of the Council Chamber, an excellent photo of him- self. I believe it is the first instance of the photo of a chairman of the Council becoming the property of the town.</p>
        <p>FOOTBALL. On Saturday Runcorn will (frost and Other things permitting) figure on the Broughton Rangers ground. Owing to the interval of compul- sory idleneas both fifteens should be in good condi- tion, and a capital game should result. My own fancy is Runcorn ; but if the Rangers play as well as they did at Runcorn, the ground. may prove the advantage, and the victory rest with them. What- ever happens, we may be sure of one thing,rd that is Runcorn are " tryers." and are hoping for championship honours yet; and when that spirit actuates the "red tails " their opponents are not consulted very much about the issue.</p>
        <p>IMPROVE YOUR WALK.</p>
        <p>BY EITSPPING INTO A PAIR OF ALF TAYLOR'S, WONDERFUL BOOTS FOR RFERYBODY TO FIT YOUR FANCY, FEST AND FINANCES. 45, HORSEMARKST STREET, 45, ,WARRINGTON. PATRONIZB YOUR OWN TOWNSMAN.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-02-08_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <head>EXAMINER, FRI DAY, FEBRUARY 8, 1901.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, FRI DAY, FEBRUARY 8, 1901.</p>
        <p>church representatives, SLI., had been accommodate I, It need hardly be said that every available epics in the sacred edifice was occupied, aud the aisles were thronged with those who were unable to find seats, while many had to be refused admission. As the con- gregation was being seiaed Mr W Grounds, organist of Si Michael's Church, played the following pieco on the organ :—Beethoven's Funeral March, Mendelssohn's Funeral March, and " All ye who weep" (Gounod). When the clergy and combined choirs had taken up their places in the chancel the service opened with "Father of Heaven whose love profound." Mr J John- son had now taken his place at the organ and he played the accoinpanimencs during the service. The choir gave an excellent rendering of Soohr's anthem " Blest are the departed," and then followed the collect offered by the Rev Canon Wood. The choir and congregation rose and joined in the hymn " Peace, Perfect Peace," the effect being at once striking and impressive. Special prayers and the grace by the Vicar concluded a service which will 'long live in the memories of those who were privileged to b 3 present. The bald nf the 2nd V.8.C.R., conducted by Bandmaster Oath wake, played the Dead March in " Saul," the congregation meanwhile standing, and the proceedings terminated with che recessional hymn "0 God, our help in ages [mit." Mr Grace, organist Holy Trinity Glau•co, played, the following voluntaries as the congregation moved out of the church : —March by Holloway; march by loyloach ; and andante, Cooper. It was estimated that there were about 1,400 people present. Widnes Free Church Council.</p>
        <p>Under the auspices of the Free Caurch Council a special memorial service was held on Saturday afternoon at the Victoria-road Wesleyan Church. The church bad been suitably draped with black material, and purple ' velvet. There was a large congregation, and many were unable to obtain admission. The service was con. ducted by tne Revs II Barraclough, D Jones, A Harvey, 1 Ii Chapman, JEI Hughes and G Bamish Saul. The Rev J Pedlar occupied the chair. Before the service C3r11111011C.41 Flanders " Di.l March " was playa I,which added considerably to the solemnity of the proceedings. After devotional exercises tha Rev G Beanhish Saul delivered an interesdag address based on the text" And ' she was a widow of about four score and four years which served Gad" (Luke ii. 37). The service was brought to a fitting conclusion by the congregation sing- ing "God save the King." St. Paul's Church, Widnes.</p>
        <p>The eelayor and Carporatiou af Widnes 'attended the %bay° chureti on Saturday at one o'ciock. The Mayor was accompanied by Colonel Hall Walker (M.P. for the division), the Mayoress (Mrs (* I Nail) Mrs H Walker,the magistrates, the members of the School Board, the Cor- porate officials; the 2ad V.B. Prince of Wales' South Lancashire Volunteers, and the 2ad V.B. St Helens Engineers, under the command of Major Lewis, Captain Sayoe, Sergeaat-Majar Williams Sergeanti Sage, and Robiason and Lieutenant Calland ; the Widnes Con- stabulary under the command of Superintendent Keigh- ley and Inspector Strickland,and the Fire Brigade under Captain Be Aley. Toe interior of the sacred edifice had been caretuly decorated for the sad occasion. The special prayer for the King brought to a close a service I which will long be rerneadtmed by, times whose privi- lege it was to obtain.admittance to the densely thronged church.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-02-08_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
        <head>FUNERAL OF THE QUEEN. Runcorn and Widnes.</head>
        <p>THE FUNERAL OF THE QUEEN. Runcorn and Widnes.</p>
        <p>Runcorn's tribute to the mcmory of our illustriou - Quern Victoria, was one wormy and fitting of the occa skin. Throughout the entire district business was sus pended from the hours of 12 noon to four or five o'clock All public buildings exhibited mourning, the Town Hall, Waterloc-road, being draped with purple, and black and white ; and the County Court offices, and the Police Buildings, not one degree behind, also exhibited their tribute of sincere respect. Memorial services were held at S% Paul's Charch, and were very largely atten- ded. United Nonconformist Memorial Service.</p>
        <p>The above was held at Sc. Paul's Cieurcti, where some 1,800 pereons assembled. Amongst them were represen- tatives of various public bodies, including Messrs 8 Taylor (president of the Runcorn Free Church Council), F J NOCIIIMII AR Norman end J Pritchard, magistrates ; Superintendent Pearson, Messrs R Posnett, J Wilson, G El Kirkham, S Walton, D Bisbrown, M J Sweetmnan, members of the Urban Council ; Messrs T L'ia, and Rey- nolds, members of Board of Guardians ; J R Frith, A Harrison, J Turnbull ; F Beck, T Simister, J NI Frith.— The pulpit and organ screen were tastefully covered with ' black and purpleicloth, and a large white wreath hung in the centre,over which were the letters "V.R.":worked in white and purple flowers, which cmapleted a very dignified tribute of mourning,The service was conducted by the Rev Alfred Wells (superintendent of the Runcorn Wesleyan Circuit assisted by the whole of the Noncon- formist ministers in the town ; and commenced with memorial music from 1 to 2 o'clock. Mr Pritchard and Mr George Bowyer presided at the organ. Punctually at two, the Revs A Wells and J o.4borne, M' .A. ascended into the pulpit, and the former gave out thehymn " 0 God our help in ages past." Mr Osborne followed with prayer, and then the vast congregation rose as the Rev II Morrison, commenced the sentences" I am the resur- rection, &amp;c.' The same minister also read Psalm sc. The hymn " Peace perfect peace in this dark world of sin," was then sung, and the Rev W A Eyre, read the lesson for the burial service let Cor. xv. 20-58. Maladies- sohn's beautiful solo " 0 rest in the Lord," then fell on the ears of ties vast congregation ; the soloist being Miss Clara Taylor. The singer did full justice to the piece, her rich voice sounding restfully and sympathetically sweet. The choir gave the anthem " What are these that are arrayed in %bite rebes,"followine an impressive prayer by the Rev J B Foster. The hymn " Rack of Ages" (a favourite of her late Majesty's) was then sung. The Rev 8 Wright (the oldest Non-conformist Minister in the town) then entered the pulpit and gave an address in which he said, they were met together under the shadow of a bereavement such as the nation had not experienced for many generations. When informed of the alarming Maims of the Queen the nation was drawn together in sympathy with the aged and suffering Sovereign, and prayer ascended to God for her Majesty's relief and recovery. It seemed for the time being tnat there was but one life in the Empire, and that was the life of their beloved Queen. We prayed " God save the Queen," and God lead saved the Queen, but in His own way. In the deep distress that afflicts our hearts, even through our tears, we can only endeavour to murmur a sad aquiesenee and sorrowfully say "Thy will be done." But the removal of our Queen from an earthly crown to , the crown of heaven has plunged her people into a sorrowing orphanhood. We have not only lest a Queen but we have also lost a sister, a mother, and a friend. The heart of the nation swells with one great throb of sorrow ; a throb, the bitterness of which we can all in a measure feel, but which no language can adequately describe. About this time, kings, nobles, and statesmen with the bereaved Royal family are met together to com- mit to the tomb all that is mortal of our departed Sovereign—there to repose until the resurrection of the just. In person we cannot be present with that august assembly, but in spirit,in sympathy, and in affectionate veneration for our Queen, we are there to place our humble tribute of grateful homage upon her honoured bier. (Hear, hear.) I venture to assert in the name of this mourning assembly than no tribute will be placed upon that bier more loyal, more sincere, or more expressive of affectionate regard for the memory of the Queen than that of the attached Nonconformists of the town of Runcern. (Hear, hear.) The Queen departs from us amidst clouds and tempest, for the shadow of hateful war still hangs over the nation ; but for the noble woman, the motherly Queen, all is tranquility and peace. Her sorrows are for ever ended, all tears are wiped away from her eyes. Personal enemies, we should suppose. the Queen had none, and all others are triumphantly vanquished and overcome. Her bier is hallowed by the tears of a sorrowing world, but her happy spirit reposes in the presence and smile of her Saviour and Lord. She has obtained the joy and felicity of the 'Good and faithful servant,' and that we may be fully assured is more than an equivalent for all the sorrows which she experienced in tier earthly pil- grimage. Take Queen Victoria for all in all, we ques- tion if ever the world will look .upon her like again."</p>
        <p>The congregation then sang " Give me the wings of faith to rise, following which the Rev A Wells took the opportunity of thanking the "United Choir," and Miss Taylor, for their efforts, and expressed the hope that the Free Church Choirs would show as much unselfish- nem and unanimity during the forthcoming "Siam! taneous Mission." (Hear, bear): The united choirs then gave a fine renditioa of the " Hallelujah Chorus," as has been the pleasure of any of thac vast congregation to have heard for many years. An impressive and historical gathering was concluded with the Benediction. pronounced by the Rev A Welts Victoria-road Board School, Runcorn.</p>
        <p>In commemoration of her late Majesty Queen Victoria, a memorial service was held on Friday afternoon in the above school. At three o'clock the 450 boys present assembled in the large schoolroom.where the service was ' conducted by Mr. J. F. Moulton, the headmaster, who, in the course of a short address to the scholars, pointed out some of the great IeSSOOS they might all learn from the long and noble life just brought to a close. He then asked the Scholars and teachers to join him with all reverence and sincerity in the service,which commenced with the funeral hymn " God rest our Queen," composed by Dean Hole, and set te music by Bertram Luard Selby The following hymns were also impressively sung by the scholars.—" i? and" On the resurrection morning." At God, our help in ages past," " Peace, tpheetfceocnt epleuaLen,' the Dead March in " Saul" was played on the pianoforte by Mr. W Vernon. Runcorn Parish Church This service, on Satarday afternoon, was preceded by a procession through several of the principal streets of the town. Shortly after one o'clock the clergy, the ' teachers, and many of the elder acholars connected with St. Michael's and Holy Trinity Churches be- tyheenrititaoasshsenable at the National Schools in Church-street where the most careful arrangements had been made for organizing the various sections of church workers, Sze The Volunteers, under the command of Captain Ashton, had in the meantime mustered strongly at the Drill Hall, and having formed up in marching order they came down Greenway-road to Devonshire-square and took their place at the head of the procession, which at length moved off via Egerton-street and Waterloo-road to the church in the following order :—Band of the 2nd V.B:C.R, officers and men of the 2nd V.8.C.R., including Captain Ashton (in command), Captain Timmins, Lieutenant Linaker, and ten sergeants, 30 band and buglers, and 86 rank and file; Mr. W. Handley (chairman of the Urban District Council), wearing his chain of office draped with purple; Messrs E Imison, I W Llghtburn, T Jones, H J Grice, J H Salkeld (members of the Council) ' • Mr F A Lake (law clerk), Dr McDougall (medical officer), E Marshall (seeretary and accountant), James Wilding (surveFer), M Barker (consulting surveyor), W Jonas (assistant secretary), T C Haddock (treasurer), and James Percival (sanitary inspector) ; members of the Ellesmere Lodge of Freemasons, viz., elegem H G Linaker, W R Wass, W Mellor. G H Sumrnerfield, T U Annett, A Sa'keld, R R Clark, H W Handley, T Hill, Joseph Tudor, R Abel, George Hailwood, Albert Abel, C Ditchfield, George Bellamy, H L Tatters, F R Birch, John Hill, with toe following visiting brethren :—Messrs J B Holland, W Frith, George Barker, Sherratt. W Farrell ; teacher of the public schools, including Mr A L Lindsay and Mr J F Moulton (headmaster)) of the National and Board Schools), representatives of the local branch of the Girls' Friendly Society, led by Mrs Maitland Wood, Church officers: St. Michael's Church, Messrs J H Weston and A Lightburn (wardens), J Sherlock, T Bowyer, R Peacock and T Clarke (sidesmen), and C H R Harrison (repre- senting trustees of St Michael's Schools) ; Mersey :Mission to seamen, Messrs W Shaw (mis.ionary),WShaw (licensed lay reader), W Shaw, jun., J Evans, Goode, J Cliffe, J Griffiths, 8 Cross and J Dutton ; Holy Trinity Church, Meagre W Fisher (warden), R Kileross, Hazelhurst, E Prince, W E Lea, J Savage, T E Garner, W Bankes, C Timmins, jun., W J Cunningham and 31 Horsefield (representing day school managers) ; Mariners' Mission Church, Messrs G W Gibson ,• twenty members of the Church Lads' Brigade, in charge of Corporal Williamson; Parish Church Schools banner, girlsirepresenting Sunday and day schools and teachers, representatives of Young Wornen's CIA sses,representati yes of Girls and Women's Guilds,representatives of Mothers Meeting,boys representing Sunday schools and teachers boys representing day schools, representatives of Young Men's Classes, representatives of Men's Class, marshal of choirs (Rev H B Firth), the vested choirs of the Parish St Michael's and Holy Trinity Churches, the clergy in their robes, viz- Revs Canon Maitland Wood (vicar), C R Killick (vicar ef Holy Trinity), W Scholes, E S Oliver. C P Bastaforth, and N J Poole; the Ringers of the Parish Church, and the Police. The marshals for the different sections were :—Miss Sewell, Mr J H Weston, Miss Maggie Smith, Messrs J Sherlock, T Brown, and John Yates. The streets were thronged with spectators as the procession moved along the selected route, and the approaches to the church were literally beseiged with the people anxious to get a place Inside as soon as the</p>
        <p>DELICIOUS MLZW&amp;TH THS, DELICIOUS MAZIWATTEH TEA DELICIOUS MAZ &amp;WATTSE TBA. SoLgAGEsts : HENRY MILLING AND Co. R10L11811,13 AND arriu. fIROCCRI ETC., 5 110ASSMARITECT-EITBET, WARRINGTON.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-02-08_WidnesExaminer_art0044" n="art0044">
        <head>Reference at Runcorn School Board.</head>
        <p>Reference at Runcorn School Board.</p>
        <p>At the monthly meeting of tho Runcorn School oled on Thursday evenieg, the Rev Canon Maltlaud Wood (chairman) referred to the death of the Queen, and said he thought the Board ought to put on record its deep sense at ths loss which the wintry had sustained. Re moved " rhat this School Boerd for Runcorn desires to place on record its deep sense of the loss sustained by ourand every institution or enterprise designed for welfare of its p oople, by ths death of our beloved the country, and the motion was carried by the Queen."—The.Rev Father Chambers seconded,Mr James Frodsham Parish Church. members sd rsiusiptopgotrote their feet.</p>
        <p>A memorial service was held in this church on Satur- day, when the Viorr, the Rev 11 B illogg, preacled to a crowded congregation. Ttia Frodsharn and He Why Volunteers, 93 rank and file, under the c immind of Major Harrison and Limt. Burton, were in attendance, as well as representatives of the Frodsham Parish Council, Messrs C E Linskar, T Esrlam, J Midge, G Rodgers, G Weave, 8 Davies, P Booth, J Barrow. j Williams, and George Jones. Prior to the service, the (Me Hibtlert) played the Dead March in At the close of the service Mr Hibbert played ofgsaanuilsrt , Beetheven's Funeral March. Trinity Wesleyan Church, Frodsham.</p>
        <p>A very solemn service was conducted on Saturday afternoon, inaugurated by the Frodsharn and District Evangelical Free Church CounCil. Mr E Rhodes presided. The, first hymn," 0 God, our help in ages past "having been sung, the Rev A II ifynasten (Primitive) read the 90th psalm, after which the Rey H Hooper (Union Church) cffered up prayer. One of the Queen's favourite hymns was then sung, " Kock of ages, cleft for me." The Chairman then alluded to the loss this country had sustained by the death of the Quinn. The lesBoa was read by the Rev G 0 Percival (Free Church), from Cor- inthian; I. born v. 20. The hymn " Peace, perfect peace, in this dark world of sin," was sung, after which the Rev T H Bailey (Wesleyan) addressed the congrega- tion. "God bless our native land "was the last hymn sung, and immediately after Mr W H Pickering played the Dead March. The Cornmunien table and pulpit were draped in black. and hanging from the book rest was a beautiful wreath of white lilies.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-02-08_WidnesExaminer_art0045" n="art0045">
        <head>Memorial Service at Earlestown and</head>
        <p>Memorial Service at Earlestown and</p>
        <p>Newton.</p>
        <p>Special services were held in almost all the places of worship in Earlestown and Newton on Saturday morning in connection with the funeral of the late Queen Victoria. All the places of business were closed from eleven o'clock until five o'clock in the evening, but the various works were closed throughout the day. Toe morning opened dull and threatening and the discomforts were greatly added to by the sloppy condition of the streets. In spite of this, however, the Various services were re- markably well att,ended and many people, who had not been inside a piece of worship before, made a point of being present on this mournful occasiOn, considering no doubt, it was a duty they owed to the memory of their beloved departed Queen. St. John's Church. The District Council decided to attend the service at St. John's Church, Earlestown, and they issued a nor- dial invitation to the tradeanvn of the town to accom- pany them, a request, which it is needless to say, was heartily acceded to. The members of the Council and officials mustered at the Town Hall at 11-45. Just as they were about to start for the church the Salvation Army accompanied by their band, passed the Town Hall on their way to the church,and the members of the Council forming in procession fell in behind, the tradesmen to the number of about 100 following on behind the Council and solemnly proceeded to the Church, where seats had been reserved. The church was packed in every part, and hundreds of people were unable to gain admittance. The service was conducted by the Revs T It and J C betatthaws (vicar and curate), and was of an impresdive character, The opening hymn was " Groat God what do I see and hear," which was followed by the Litany. The next hymn was "God of the living, pa whose eves," and following this was a portion of the service for the burial of the dead. Other hymns were " Christ will gather in Hie own," and " 0 God, our help in ages past," concluding with the prayers of thanks- giving for the late Queen and for the King. The whole congregation joined heartily in the service, an 1 all stood whilst Mr Harrison, the organist, played, the " Dead March " in Saul. 1 Wargrave Church.</p>
        <p>The attendance at this church was large, and the ser- vice consisted] principally of that for the burial of the dead. The opening hymn was " flow sweet the name of Puns sounds,'' and the other by was " Lesd Kindly Light." The choir sang very impressively the anthem " Blest are the departed who in the Lord are sleeping henceforth for evermore," and at the close of the service the Rev. J. Carson gave a Short address bsar- ing upon the life and attributes of the departed Queen. A collection was afterwards made in aid of the Soldiers and Sailors Fund for the relief of families of those serv- lag in South Africa. At the close the organist, Mr Ley- land played" The Dead March." During the collection, a lady overcome with emotion, fainted in the Church, and some difficulty was experienced in restoring her. • Earlestown Wesleyan Chapel. _ _ _</p>
        <p>On Sunday evening the Rev C E D.)Ve the resident minister conducted a special memorial service to the memory of the late Queen Victoria, in the Wesleyan Chapel. The service was opened by the playing of the Dead March by the organist, Miss Green&amp;ll, after which a solo was rendered by Miss If Halliday "0 Rest in the Lord" The hymn "Leader of Faithful Souls and Guide was next sung by the congregation and after a short prayer the choir rendered the anthem " What are these arranged in White." The reverend .dainister read for the first lesson the 31st chapter of the book of Pro- ' verbs beginning at the 10th verse and at the ,conclusion of this, the solo "I know that my Red eemer liveth ' Wag given by Miss G Roberts. The second lesson was taken from Revelations the 19th chapter beginning at the</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-02-08_WidnesExaminer_art0046" n="art0046">
        <head>C RAW FORD'S</head>
        <p>C RAW FORD'S</p>
        <p>CREAM CRACKERS, CRISP AND ('RE AMY</p>
        <p>6th verse and the hymn " Brief life is here our portion was afterwards sung by the congregation The preacher took for his text the 29th verse ot the 31st chapter of the book of Proverbs' Many daughters have done virtuously ; but thou excelleit them all," and remarked that these words were suggested to his rniud ately he was informed one ev,noig that our tploved Queen had passed away. When it was decided to hold snernorlal service in tnat Cnapel, he resolved that he would 1133 them for his text. He did riot tnink that if he looked from one end ot the scripture to the other he could find words that were more suited to the occasion. Uu thea delvered an eloque,it parP.lvric on the Queen.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-02-08_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <head>WARRINGTON'S rRISJTE TO THE DEPARTED MONARCH. A Day of Mourning.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON'S rRISJTE TO THE DEPARTED MONARCH. A Day of Mourning.</p>
        <p>Warrington was in deepest mourning on Saturday. Never before have there been such general signs of mourning in the Borough. Everywhere were to be seen iadicatioas of the national bereavement. Business was en- tirely suspended tor the day,witti one or two exceptions, and the town wore the quiet aspect of a Sunday. All the churches where memorial services were held were filled to overflowing, and oa every hand there were deep manifestations of grief. There was a muffled tolling of bells throughout the morning, blinds were lowered in every house, and black was almost universally worn by men, women and ceildren. To make the day more notable, the Mayor (Alderman R )berts) seized the oppor- tunity of publicly proclaiming the King immediateiy at the close of the services. All the business establishments were closed as a last tribute of respect to the venerated Sovereign many of the business places were drap)d, and at all the works, flags fluttered in the breeze at half-mast. At many of the shops shutters draped in black were put up, an i in numerous windows portraits of Queen Victoria were exhibited. Messrs Duttou's estaolishmeat was very effectively draped and over the principal entrance the words "She was good to the poor" were printed in letters of Royal purple on a black ground. Tne front' portion of the premises of Messrs R Garaett and Sons, and Messrs B,ydeil Bros was artistically festooned with cloth of Royal purple, surmounted with six weaths of evergreens with the letters "VR " in the centre of each There were also effective displays at Mr J Marsh's and the Pelican Inn, Buttermarket-street. The windows of the Conservative Club were covered with the letters'' V. R." with a deep border of black, while affixed to the iron rejig of the balcony outside was the motto " Victoria the Good," with a portrait of the late Queen in the centre. Below in the window of Messrs. Jones' shop, were two excel- lent portraits of the departed monarch 811r- rounded with deep borders of black. Toe iron rails at the top of the Town Hall steps were draped with cloth of royal purple and black. At 11.30 the Mayor and Cerporation. offi fiats and general public assembled at the Town Hall and after- wards walked in processional order to the Parish Church. At the head of use procession was the reghnen- tal band of the let Volunteer Battalion (Prince of Wales's) Volunteers, followed by the bugle, bend, the new mounted infantry company, the cyclist section, and the volunteers about 500 strong. They were under the direction of Colonel Ridgway (cemmaadiag the corp), the other officers present being the Adjutant (Major Boerne), Major Buckton, Captains Eleisten, J D Fair- cough, and B Fairclough, Lieutenants Smith, dough Willcocks, Roberto Lieutenant and Qu‘rter-elaeter Potter and Surgeon-Captain Adams. Behind the Volun- teers walked the members of the Ambulasne Corps, under Captain Waring and Superintendent Baldry, amongst whom were a number of men dressed in khaki, who have recently returned horse from service in the hospitals in South Africa. Tree Post Office carps, the fire brigade, and a police detachment followed. Imme- diately in front of the Mayor were the mace and sword ' bearers. The Mayor, whose robes had been draped for the occasion, was accompanied by his chaplain (the Rev J Yoage) and the town clerk (Mr J Lyon Whittle). Walking behind were the Deputy Mayor, the hohorary freemen of the borough, members of the Cerperation, magistrates, guardians of the poor, public officials, representatives of friendly societies, and the general Amongst. the members of the C orporatioa present were Aldermen Belton,Francomb, Shaw Green, Heaton, Hutchinson, Monks and Sutton, Councillors Baird, Brough, Barton, Davies, Duncan, Ellison, Hulse, Alfred Jolley, Malkin, Peacock, Pierpoiat, Plurnptoa, .;ohn Roberts, Rogers, Sampson, Shillcock, Smethurst, and Wilkinson- The magistrates present were Messrs Thomas Birchall, John Fairclough, Dr Fox, John Geddes, Dr Gorman, J W Hayward,Jamesliepherd,F Mooke,Henry Thornton, and S Wright. There was a good muster of the members of the Board of Guardians and officials. The two lady members of the Board—Mrs Clay and Mrs Edelsten—were present, and they were the only two ladies in the procession. They were escorted by Mr Arthur 11 Crosfield. The Post Office staff was strongly represented, amongst those present being Mr Viney (post-master) and Mr Broadhurst (chief clerk) Amongst the other bodies represented were the Licensed Victuallers and the Friendly Societies, The Manchester Unity of Othifellows was represented by Mr John Eason and Mr Ralph Norman. whilst the Druids had represen- tatives in Mr David Houghton, and Mr Edwin Allen. A large number of the general public joined the pro- cession.</p>
        <p>The route of the procession to the Parish Church was thickly lined with spectators. The procession passed through the central gates at the Town Hall, and as the long line of leading townsmen dressed in mourning slowly wended its way to the Parish Church to pay the last token of respect to the departed Sovereign, the spectacle was a deeply impressive one. The procession was met at the gates by the clergy and choir. Ia the absence of the rector, through illaesi, the service was conducted by the Rev A 8 06a:won and the Rev E J Willcocks. Accommodation had been reserved for those in the procession, and on entering the sacred building it was found that the galleries were already crowded, while many of the seats below were occupied by the officers and men stationed at Orford Barracks, which is the depot for the King's (Liverpool Regimeat),the Prince of Wales's Vo!untelr (South Lancashire R giment), and the permanent staffs of the 3rd and 4th Battalions the King's (Liverpool Regiment) and the 3rd Battalion the Prince of Wales's Volunteers (South Lancashire Regi- ment). The military did no: join in the civic proces- sion. The service maned with the singing of the hymn "The Saints of God ! their conflict past," Chen followed the Lord's Prayer and the opening sentences of " The Order for the Burial of the Dead," and Psalm xc. was chanted. Succeediag it was the lesson (taken out of the 151 Chapter of the former Epistle of 8: Paul to the Corinthians), which was read Dy the Rev E J Willcocks, headmaster of the Grammar School. Toe congregation then joined in singing the hymn com- mencing "Now the labourer's task is o'er," and many were visibly affected. At the close of an impressive service, the Volunteer Band played the Dead March in "Saul." This was followed by the singing of the National Anthem and the service concluded: Mr. Darby presided at the organ with his accustomed ability The procession was reformed outside the church and proceeded to the Town Hall. A reed of praise Is due to the Chief Constable and his staff for the excellent marshalling of the procession.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-02-08_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <head>PROCLAIMING THE KING AT WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>PROCLAIMING THE KING AT WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>On returaing to the Town Hall, the Mayor divested himself of all signs of mourning, and, accompanied by members of the Council and magistrates, took up a position on the steps of the Town Hall. The lawn .n front of the building was filled with a dense mass of people. The proceedings were also witnessed by • num- ber of people from the windows of buildings across from the Park gates, while several adventurous spectators se- cured places of vantage among the branches of the large trees on th 3 left of the lawn. The!volunteers, who lined the front of the hall, gave a general salute, and the buglers afterwards blew a fan- taronade. The Town Clerk called for silence in the worth " Oyez ! Oyez! Oyez I Let all persons keep silence whilst his Worship the Mayor proclaims His Majesty," and the Mayor then read the proclamation in a firm clear voice as follows :</p>
        <p>" Whereas it tau pleased Almighty God to call to His mercy our late Sovereign Lady Queen Victoria, of blessed and glorious memory, by whose decease the Im- perial Crown of the United Kingdom of Great Britain and Ireland is solely and rightfully come to the High and Mighty Prince Albert Elward ; we, therefore, tha lords spiritual and temporal of this realm, being here assistad with these of Her late Majesty's Privy Council, with numbers of other principal gentlemen of quality, with the Lord Mayor, aldermen,and citizens of London. do now hereby,with one voice and consent of tongue and heart publish and proclaim that the High and Mighty Prince, Albert Edward, is now, by the death of our late Soverein, of happy memory, become our only lawful and rightful Liege Lord Edward tbe Seventh by the 'Grace of God, King of the United Kingdom of Great Britain and Ireland, Mender of the faith, Emperor of India: To whom we do acknowledge all faith and con- stant obedience, with all hearty and humble affection, beseeching God, by whom kings and queens do reigs, to bless the Royal Prince Edward the Seventh, with long and happy years to reign over us. " Given at the Court of Si James's, this twenty-third day of January, in the year of our Lord one thousand nine hundred and one." At the close of the words" God Save the King " the buglers repeated their fanfaronade, and the call for cheers for the King by the Mayor was loudly responded to. As the cheers subsided, the Mayor called for .• cheer for the King's Consort, and the crowd again cheered heartily. Then the volunteer band played the National Anthem, the people joining in. More cheers were given, and the proceedings ended.</p>
        <p>DBLICIOUS MAZIWATTHS TB&amp; DBLICIOLTS MaZiWATCHB DHLIMUS MA.ZA.WATTHII TBA Soz Aeions : MOIR! HILLINCI AWD 00, LTD. WRoLVIALE AND lISTAIL 0110CDERS 6. FloseinuarocreraszT, wAtuuserOs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-02-08_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <head>Wycliffe Congregational Church.</head>
        <p>Wycliffe Congregational Church.</p>
        <p>Memorial servicos were held on Sunday (m)ruing and evening). The pulpit, whicn had ben drap .d and fes- tooned with black and royal purple, was occupied by the pastor. the Rev. J Yong,s. A.; the motning service the hymns used were those known to be her late Majesty's tavourities, and a special memorial hymn had been composed by Mr A Conley, which was sung very effectively by the congregation. The following are the lines ; Rest to our noble Queen !</p>
        <p>God's faithful servant in tho midst of s'Zrife. The cares of Empire laid for ever down, She gales from atin this fadeless crown of life. "Peace, perfect peace at last !" Life's pathway darkeas irs the twilight gray. But Is Us premace there ii perfect peace Where sunrise broaaens into perfect day. "Nearer, oh God, to Thee !" Though roar' the path, and dssolate the night. 'he Cross of Jesus shone amidst tha gloom To guide His servant to tke realms of light. For ever with the Lord ! " Oh blest Caminunion, fellowship Divine ! :hi, loved ones greet thee at the Saviour's side, For they are Christ's, and Christ for even thine. " Thy will, oh God, be done ! " We falter, but Thy pertect will is best ; Grant ua, oh Corist, in life to serve fties still, Then in Thy Love eternally to rest.</p>
        <p>The</p>
        <p>The Rev J Yang' sp Ike at the evening service on " nation of mourners," choosing for his texts the words "And the land shell mourn every family" and •`I bowed down mourning, as one that bewaileth his mother." He said throughout the length and breadth of these king- dome there was the unrivalled sense of real and pro- found bereavement and sorrow. The m naming was in every home. Thera had never been anything like it in tnis country before. may would remember the death of the Prince Consort, wean the ristion serrowed with the Queen, but the sorrow then Was more sympathetic than personal. The sorrow th at day was far greater, , more real, more universal than was the case then. Al- ' though the nation sympathised with the Royal family, it also felt itself bereaved of the queenly mind and heart of one who proved herself the mother of her people. Way was it that all ranks of people concurred in exhibi- tions of mourning which were expensive alike in time, trouble and cost? Not merely b-3cause the Queen was dead, or that the monarch was dead who had reigned longer than any other in Elglish history. All this was beciuse, to a matchless per- formance of 83 years of sovereign duty, Victoria always joined an equally unrivalled display of sovereign sympathy ! She ruled for love ; she reigned in sympathy, flow much sweeter, kinder, better,would a world like this soon become if all hearts became as un- selfish as hers. Queen though she was, lifted by her position far ab eve all her people, herself out of actual sight of the needy and the suffering and the distressed, far removed from it ; but she never forgot, overlooked, or shut the eye of her imagination. As surely as any calamity betel, any sudden catastrophe heppened, any need arose, she was the earliest of inquirers, the readiest of helpers, the most thoughtful in affection and sym- pathy. Every philanthropic movement during her reign received her personal symplehy. Warrington was to-day enjoying the benefits of tne order of nurses which she took suith delight in establishing in celebration of her Jubilee. They could not forget how she devoted £70,000 of the women's Jabilee offering to that benevolent object. Tney knew,of ceurse,the school in which Qeeen Victoria leerned the art of sympathy. Beginning life with a sensitive and unselfish nature,she had the feeling and theer soa glathY, developei in the sad school of personal as Yraorrow. Never in the bitterest days p of grief, did the Queen neglect any duties of her office. The Queen's official work had ever been astonishing to suoh as had had any knowledge ec it. see kept herself perfectly acquainted with all that was transpiring both in this and other countries, and none could eveer say that she permitted her own preferences, or prejudices, or sorrows to hinder ter con- stant discharge of multifarious duty. But her sorrows did most beautifully chasten her. By the grace of Ged they made her to become in such fulness the m ether ol her people, and enabled her to attract and retain the affeetion (Weis of the humblest mid obscurest peor. Those were some of the manifestations of that sympathetic care which the Queen always had displayed. It was a life of that rare high order that had won for her so wide- spread and so profound a veneration and love. The marvel would be, not that her death should be thus pro- foundly mourned, but that it were not ! It was no con- ventional sorrow, no display of courtly sycophancy, that made the whole nation and the Empire put on sable attire, and assemble in hundreds and thousands around ;he ns march's grave. The" land had mourned, every family" of it, and multitudes had " bowed down mourning as those who bewailed their mother." They thanked God with all their hearts for such a wondrous life, such a marvellous inflaence, such a noble example Seitable hymns were sung. The choir sang the anthem "Abide with ma " (Bernby), and a quartette " Yes though I walk" (Sullivan) was contributed by Mrs R Pearson, Mrs A. Longshsw, Mr A Griffiths and Mr W H Wallington.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-02-08_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <head>United Service in Bold-street Wesleyan Church.</head>
        <p>United Service in Bold-street Wesleyan Church.</p>
        <p>There was a very large congregation at Bold-street Wesleyan Caurch at noon on Saturday, when a united memorial servioe in coanection with the Free Churches of Warrington and district was held. The rostrum was I draped in purple and black, and three vases oentainin,g beautiful white flowers had been placed on the com- munion table. The following ministers were present : The Revs T Bishop (Bold-street Wesleyan), 0 a Hocken Bewsey-read Wesleyan), J S Hughes (Hill Cliffs Baptist), J T Schofield (Golborae-screet Baptist), C Finch (Legh-street Primitive Methodist), J F Idartnews (Latchford Baptist). The service commenced with the sin.ine of the hymn," 0 God, our help in ages past," Mr F Hickman presiding at the organ. Prayer having been offered by the Rev J F Matthews. the Rev J S Hughes read the sentences commencine,, " I am the resurrection and the life," after which the Rev J T Schofield read Psalm xxxlx., and Mr A Corney Palm xc. The lesson (St Paul's Epistle to the Corinthians, chapter xv ) was read by the Rev 011 Hocken, and the choir effectively render&amp; the anthem "The radiant morn has passed sway." The Rev T Bishop, superintendent ef the circuit, then delivered an appropriate address. He said that our be- loved Q nen, the great and good Vinoria, was no more. She had gone the way of all flesh, in obedience to the , I law of mortality, which made no exceptions, not even , ,to Queens rani rulers. Yet she was not dead, for she I would live long in the hearts of those who loved her I whilst she ruled them, and whose service of love was I cheerfully given. The heart of the nation went out to- wards the young Queen 63 years ago when she was crowned, and it remained with her to the end. So I intimately had she by her tact, tier loving sympathy, and her spontaneous generosity made the sorrows and joys of the nation hers, that with quick response the whole nation to its farthest bounds had made her joys and sorrow their personal concern, and when the much- I loved Queen bowed in sorrow over the dead Prince Con- ; sort the whole nation wept sympathetic tears. Also, when the great and good Queen rejoiced, all the people I were made glad likewise. She came to the throne at a I time when loyalty was at a very low ebb, and lingered alone in the hearts of the upper classes. The spirit of discontent was abroad, and the blazing stacks in the orMotry and the wild Chartism in the towns showed that the hope of anarchy and revolution ran riot in the minds of the people. The goodness of the late Queen, her tact sal kindliness, her purity of life, and above all her constant making the welfare of her people of primary consideration, had changed all that, and it would be difficult to find now a more loyal and law-abiding people anywhere than the British nation was to-day. They cansid3red that the personal character of their Queen had brought about this, and had been the con- stant leaven which had worked to bring about that result. Her first words when informed that she was Queen of Great Britain were addressed to the Arch- bishop :" I bog your Grace to pray for ma." Thus the reign was begun in prayer. In her speech, on taking the oaths at the Privy Council, she said" I should feel utterly oppressed by the burden imposed upon me were I not sustained by the hope that the Divine Providence which has called me to this work will give me strength for the performance of it." And the reign had been continued in prayer, for their late Queen's religion was no perfunctory performance, but tne sweet communion of a sacred soul with its great and loving Father. The secret of her greatness was her faith in God, and her constant endeavour to rule with Godlike justice kind- ness, and loving sympathy. Her personal character was ever above suspicion. Many men had assailed the principles of the monarchy,and in leud and strident tones had said many things derogatory to the office, things very repugnant to those who believed in a limited monarchy, but no one had ever dared to attack the character of their late beloved and virtuous Queen. No one had ever ventured to expose himself to thewrath and fury of the crowd by daring to indulge in innuen- dos against her. So well was she known to be on the side of everything that was pure and unblameable, so well was it understood that she would not tolerate any- thing that was questionable and that she would not have in her Court, nor near her presence, anyone of doubtful reputation nor anyone upon whom the breath of suspicion had fallen, but would always maintain and uphold the purity and virtuous reputation of her Court. that anyone daring to hint the contrary would in any public assembly have bean hissed and turned out, whilst so popular on that account was their beloved Queen with the people that in an ordinary crowd such a ealunoiaa- tor would have been torn in pieces. The subjects of the kingdom spoke many languages and dialects, whilst there was much variety in appearance, dress, manners, custom, and so forth, but in all the lands that belonged</p>
        <p>DELIOIOIIB MAZAWATTIDI TEA DELICIOUS MAEAWATIIIII TEA DELICIOUS MAZAWATTHH TEA Eove AGENTS: HENRY MILLING Awl, 0&lt;,., LTD, WHOLESALE AIM IieTAIL GROCERS ETC. 5, HORSEXAREST.STRELT, WARRINOT.N.</p>
        <p>- to us, and amongst all the peoples and tribes that acknowledged our Sovereign as their temporal head, out , late great and beloved Q seen was ever known as • • Christian znonarch,a just ruler, a friend of the oppressed , a good and a pure woman. Perhaps the goodness and , purity of her life bad as much to do with the welding of the nation into oaa concrete kingdom as • anything else they could think about. An °goo. , site character would have sown discord, created dissension, and the elements of disintegration would soon have accomplished the ruin of the Empire. Under her pure and just administration we had had unbounded success, internal and external, enlarged territory, increased comfort, multiplying riches, and growing security. The record of the reign could not be dealt with then,but they could all thank God for having given them so good a Queen at such a time.and having spared her to them so long. The news of her illness was a sur- prise, but the tidings of her death was a great shoal. Sue had lived so long, they almost hoped she might live for ever. Outside the circle of loving children, grand- children, and great-grandchildren, an unseen crowd made up of many nationalities--Canadians,Australiaas Indians, Burmese, Africans, and the rest of our vast Empire—stood and wept bitter tears of heart-felt sorrow as they gathered in the room where the great and good Victoria breathed her last on earth and first in heaven. From all parts came tidings of universal regret, and some of the incidents showed how much the late Queen had endeared herself to all classes. In Africa a Kaffir exclaimed, " The great Queen dead ! Then we are all dead." In Birmingham, large crowds, composed of the rich and of the very poorest, gathered around the statue of the Queen and silently gazed on the well- known face, whilst a great space was railed off and covered with wreaths of beautiful flowers. One touching scene in Dublin was observed by a gentleman who vouched for the truth of it. A ragged boy, a very ragged wild little Irish lad, was stopped in his progress by the tidings on a board outside a newsethop "Death of the Queen.' He paused long, trying to realize it. Doubt- less he had often seen her on her last visit and had got to know her kindly face. He was full of sorrow, and searched his pocket,and found one coin—a penny—with which he went and purchaaed a bunch of violets and then pinned them over the word "Queen 'on the board bursting into tears and sobbing loudly. She had the knack of touching all hearts, old and young, rich and poor. The beauty of her domestic life alike appealed to the nation, and its influence was an assurance for good ,in the rule ef him who had succeeded her. ,They bowed in sorrow that day. She was gone, full 'of old age, honour, with the world's respect and an Empire's love. Yet she lived, lived in the brightest memory to stimu- late to goodness, to exhort to purity, an example for all alike. They thanked God who gave them such a Queen as Victoria the Great and Good! The address was fol- lowed by the singing of the hymn " Rock of Ages." After prayer, by the Rev C Finch and the singing of " Give me the wings of faith to rise " the service con. cluded with the Benediction.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-02-08_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>St. Paul's Church.</head>
        <p>St. Paul's Church.</p>
        <p>A memorial service was h aid in this church at noon. The congregation were called together by a beautifully rung peal of mufliod bells. The service chosen was the regular Burial Service, ocnittine, of coarse, the commitsl prayer and adding hymns ten. Punctually at eleven o'clock the choir slowly proceeded down the north aisle and up the centre aisle to their stalls,the assistant clergy (the Rev L W Thomas and D Kerrin) and the Vicar (the Rev E R Tarback) following. As the procession meved round the church the opening sentences of the Burial Service were intonee by the Vicar, who conducted the entire service. Then followed Psalm xc., the lesson, • hymn' sentences, anthem " Blessed are the depare ted," from Gaul's paasioa music, sung by Mr F Scatter- good and a quartette of male voices. The sentence " I heard a voice, kee," was then intoned, and the prayers concluded this part of the service. A hymn followed, and after the blessing Chopin's Funeral March was played, and the singing of " God save the King" brought tha solemn proceedings to a conclusion. Emmanuel Church, Bold-street. In respense to a request by the presiding Bishop of the Reformed Church to all similar churchee in England, a special memorial service was held en Saturday in Emmanuel Church,at 2 p.m • tee hour appointed tor the funeral service of the late Queen in St. Geerge's Chapel, Windsor. There was a good attendance and the service was one of great solemnity. To hymns sung were " Lord, it belongs not to my care whether I die or live," 1" How sweet the hour of closing day," and "Rock of Ages, cleft for me." The service was conducted by the Rev E T Reed, who also preached a short sermon from the text " It is better to go to the noose of mourning." He said : A little over forty years have passed since the great outburst of national mourning took place on the occasion of the death of Prince Conaort,and at this very bout in the beautiful St. George's Chapel, Windsor, the earthly remains of the beloved Queun and widow, amid even wider spread lamentations, are the centre of mournful interest in that great and illustrious assembly. And we, too, gatnerecl :together for this solemn service, would join them in spirit in paying our tribute of respect to the memory of our late beloved Queen. The wise man says "It is better to go to the house of mourning than to the house of feast- ing," and if this be true then we have done well to come here to-day. But why is it better ? It is because on such occasions we are reminded that some day a similar call will corns to us to depart out of this world unless the Master shall first corns again. This fact some are inclined too lightly to put on one side, and so to pass through life unprepared for that heaven from which all sin is excluded ' • but just as the making of a will doe. not in any way hasten a man's death, so neither does preparation for heaven cause death to Come sooner than it otherwise would. To the Queen God granted a long life and an honoured old age, and now she rests from her labours and her w)rks do follow her. Io permitting her to reign over as so long and so beaefiemotly we owe our heartfelt thanks to our Heavenly Father, who, in the evening of her life has taken her to Himself. With hearts bowed in submission to His will we say, "The Lord gave and the Lord bath taken away, blessed be the name of the Lord."</p>
        <p>At the close of the sernonn prayers were offered for the King and the 'Royal Family, and the congregation remained standing while the Dead March was played by the organist, Miss Eva Lela. At bsth services on Sunday last reference was made to the accession of King Edward VII., and the National Anthem was sung n each occasion.</p>
        <p>St. Peter's Church A special memorial service was held in this church on Saturday at twelve o'ctock,the building being completely filled. The service used was the one ordered by his Majesty's Privy Council. The hymns " Days and moments," and "0 God, our help in ages past," were beautifuliy rendered by the choir. At the close of the service the Dead March in •• Saul " was played by lir Peter Edgar. The service was conducted by the Rev W Bracecamp, curate in-charge. Christ Church, Latchford.</p>
        <p>A well-attended memorial service was held in the above place of worship on Saturday afternoon. The service WAS conlucted by the Vicar, the Rev F A Screeton, and special prayers were offered for the King At the conclusion of the service, the organist, Mr Pus played Mendelsaohn's Funeral March.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-02-08_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <head>St. James' Church, Latchford.</head>
        <p>St. James' Church, Latchford.</p>
        <p>An impressive Tuna:wrist service was conducted in St. James' Church, Lstchford, in the morning by the Rev F E Powell. Snecials prayers were offered and appropri• ate music rendered, the hymns includinz Now the labourer's task is o'er " and " On the resurrection morning.' &amp; notable feature of the service was the large I number of communicantl. The offartory was for the sick and poor fund. A similar service tcy.4 place in the evening.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-02-08_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>Holy Trinity Ohurch,</head>
        <p>Holy Trinity Ohurch,</p>
        <p>The serviee was largely attended. A beautiful wreath was hung in front of the pulpit. The opening sentences of the Burial Service were chanted by the chair in pro- cession. The form of service was the ordinary Burial Service, and after the lessen the Rev E C E Carleton delivered an address. The conoludingjaymn was' Now the labourer's task is o'er," and at the close of the ser— vice the orgsnist (Mr Percy Randall) played the Dead March in " Saul."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-02-08_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>ST. HELENS.</head>
        <p>ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>Suaoteas &amp;Eat £115. &amp;bout eleven o'clock on Fri- day night a sensational robbery was perpetrated at Messrs Cannington and Shaw's bottle works, St Helens, a bag containing £ll5 being taken from the office of the under-manager. The emplol63 at these works are always paid on Friday, but there are a number of night bands who are paid about 5.30 on Saturday morning, and it was the money for these men which was stolen. The office where the money was kept was in charge of • trustworthy employe who has been connected with the works for a great number of years. While he was away giving instructions in another part of the works, the Inak of his office door was picked, and an entrance effected. On the robbery being discovered the police ; were communicated with, but up to the present they I have got no clue.</p>
