        <p>The charms of " Home " g with us, whercver duty or plcasure calls, whether in peac"ful occupations, or in the stern necessi'des of war. How intensely precious must " Home" be to those who are retnrning from the struggles and hardships endured in Scuth Africa. Think of the sick and wounded invalided " Home," to the care of mother. wife, or friends, who are only too anxious to have their dear ones with them again, to nurse them back into a new life of perfect health and strength. At such a time, the great recuperative Tonic is Gwilyrn Evans' Quinine Bitters. It gives power to the weak limbs, it steadies the nerves, brightens the eye, infuses new hope and builds up the system anew. Many patients that have been suff ^ring from Enteric and other wasting diseases, simply r'quire to be systematically I treated with a tonic restorative. A complete rest and a reviving tonic, is all they require. In Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters, this will be found in its highest perfec- tion. The Bs Remrdy for Loss of Appetite, Indigestion. Impure Blood, Chest Diseases, and Influenza.</p>
