      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-02-08_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>BEFORE the MAGISTRATES</head>
        <p>BEFORE the MAGISTRATES</p>
        <p>AN UNDESIRABLE ARRANGEMENT. RUNCORN PUBLICAN FINED. At the Runcorn Petty Sessions on Monday, before Messrs, F J Norman and W. Timmins, a case was heard in which John William Fitzsimmons, landlord of the "Friendship Tavern." F. yer-street, was summoned for having on the 26th January, sold intoxicating liquor to a drunken person named Paul Caldwell.—Mr. A.Fletcher ot Northwioh. prosecuted for the police, and Mr. J. W. Knowles, Widnes, defended.—From the evidence of Sergeant W. Brewster, Acting Sergeant S. Gregory, and John Simmett (a boy in the employ of Mr. Caradnc, pawnbroker) it would appaar that the officers saw Caldwell in the streets tha worse for liquor. Sergeant Brewster also saw a couple of the name of Cooke. who were the worse for liquor, and followed them into the Friendship Tavern to see whether they would eerye them and to warn the land- lord. Ho was accompanied by Gregory. The female Cooke NVS9 Simply asking about lodgings when he got there, close on her heels; lent he and Gregory found three men there, named Caldwell, Farren, and Whittle, with liquor before them. Caldwell was unmistakably drunk. P, was pointed out that the landlord of the Friend6hip Tavern was also the landlord of the Lodging Flouse, and had aPpointed Whittle as his deputy. As the inn and tho Indlieg-house were next to each other, na`ur...:y the police had great difficulty in supervising the piss —For the defence it was unsuccessfully argued that Caldwell was not drunk when served, and a list of the arnmint of ale he had censumed that day was sub- mitted.Cader the close cross-examination of Mr Pletcher, however, Caldwell admitted having more drink than was accounted for in the list sohmitted.—The Chairman in Illposing e fine of £2 and £3 coats, said the Bench considered it was a very had system that one man should hold a licence for the inn and also for the lodging house, and they wished to call the attention of the Superintendent of the Police, and also the District Council (1-hrouvh Mr W Handley, who was present) to the rnatter.—Suhsequently Paul Csldw(ll, boatman, was fined 5; and 10a costs or 14 days, for drunkenness on licensed premises.</p>
        <p>THE ROBBERIES PROM A LOCK UP SHOP—" BILL SYKE3 AND OLIVER TWIST." Mr. F Mock; and other magistrates had before them at the Warrington Police Court on Monday, two cases of thefts from the lock-no shop of Samuel Silcock, second-hand dealer, 29, Cartwright-street. In the first inetance a boy named George Davidson, residing in Carwrielet-street, was charged with stealing a saucepan value 2+., from the sh,p, at 2.30 on Monday afternoon —The Chiet Constable stated the facts of the ease, from which it appeared that some time ago the boy borrowed the saneep-sn from a woman saying that his mother wanted ir. Be at once sold the pan to Mr. Silcock,and last Monday, the woman Ircorning suspicious, de- mander! the return of it. The boy decided to steal the pan back attain, and effected an entrance to the shop, during Mr. Silcock's absence. The theft was r. ported to the police.—George Davidson, of Dalton Bank, uncle to the first prisoner. was then placed in the dock, and both were charged with stealing from the same chop on the 11th January, a cardigan jacket, a pair os skate.. a cpantity of oranges, a pair of gloves, a pair of shoea mnd a pair of clogs, amounting in value to EL —The Chief Constable said that the prisoners entered the shop bv breaking a hoard which was nailed across a broken pane in the back window, and the articles enumerated in the charge were stolen. When apnrchen- Ard the etter prisoner was wearing the cardigan jacket, and a portion of the gloves was found in his posaesshm. The rest of the prnnory had been disposed of by the younger prisoner When charged !hey admitted having committed the offer:oe jointly,—The boy now informed the Bench that the elder prisoner pushed him through the window, and he atnle the gooda.—The Chief Constable : It seems we have Bill Sykes and Olver Twist even in these days. Continuing Mr Talbot said he was of opinion that the man had been exercising an influence tom the hey in the matter.—Tbe Bmeh ordered the boy to receive twelve stroke. from the birch and to remain in the cells until six o'clock. and the elder prisoner was sentenced to three months' imprisonment with hard labour. ATTEMPT TO STAB A. STALLHOLDER IE THE MARKET. - At the fiornoeh Police Court, on Saturday, before Mr Birchall fin the chair) and other magistrates, a man who when asked he the police for his name, told them to call him" John Blueeown," and refused any, further infor- mation about himself, was charged with making use of threats towards Genre!, Stevenson, a stallholder in the market. on Friday afternoon. George Stevenson, living at 18, P:nner's brow, said he Was a greengrocer, and had stall in the market. On Friday afternoon he was serving a lady, when an orange drooped off the stall. The lady told him, and he turned away to cerve someone eta when priaoner picked on the ()ranee Wittletle stoke to him and uaid "Give me that orange," when Bluegown said " I did not steal the orange" WitneA got the ,range _from him and thoneht no more about it. He was vetoing another relator-nee when prisoner came up with a knife Prianner threatened to atab him in the back, and if he had not turned round would have done an. He reported o'ha matter to the polico.—P.C. Clough said that at 12 30 on Friday afternoon be Wee on duty in the marker when he saw Ph prisoner loitering annut in ruepicieue manner. Be received a complaint about Prisoner and took him into custody. At the Police 8-einn the mar,., conduct was disgr,c4nl —The Bench 'hounsl the man over in two sureties of £lO to keep the ea, sac,. or, in default, Oise month's imprisonment. He went to gaol.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-02-08_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>MINOR OASES.</head>
        <p>MINOR OASES.</p>
        <p>-- Warrington Borough Court. _</p>
        <p>THURSDAY —Before Mr. John Paircinugh (in the chair). rouncillors Plerpelnt and Sme,hurst, and air. J Rymer Young. TEM Cent ov H. B. PIIII,Urg" —On the name of Harry Bolton Furness being called, the Clerk explained that the ea.e was adjourned a month ago the defendant being charged with arrears of maintenance due to his wife.arnounting with the costs to £3 11s Ed Mr Browne appeared f r the com- plainant and the defendant was represented by Mr. Steel— Mr. BTOWne said the case was adjmrned td see if the defendant would pay any of the money. He had paid nothing whatever an be (Mr. ',myrtle) had had notice that there was going to be an application to vary the order.— Mr. Steel, on behalf of the defendant, said an order was made on November 23rd for defendant to pay El per week. Since tr en he had hoer) out of work,with the exception of one night when he was employed in a b4tehruse and received 3s. At the time the order was made defendent was the</p>
        <p>~</p>
        <p>CADBURY'S Coco.s. on the testimoni of the Lancet "represents the tandard of highest purlty.” It is entirely •ree from all fo stgt. substances, quell as kola, Malt. hops, *c.. nor its alkal used to darken the colour (and to deceive the eve). Dr. Andrew Wilson. In a recent article in the Illustrated London News writes : "Cocoa is In itself a perfect toed. VA r4qui*es no addition of drugs whatever." CAD - SEMI'S COCOA Is absolutely pure, and should its taken by old and young. at all times and in all seasons ; for Children 14 it an Heal beverage, promoting bealtby growth and development in a remarkable degree. Insist on haring CADBURY'3, as other COMAS are often substituted far the sake of extra profit. Sold only in Packets and Tins.-1 Advt. (3) .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-02-08_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <p>l'qtaidator for Furness and Company and it was stated that his wages were £2 fis. Since then the firm had gone into banks uptcy and another liquidator was appointed When the case same tefore the Court four weeks ago the defendant was medically easmined and certified to be insane He was sent to the Workhouse for 14 days but made his I escape. He Was re-arrested and taken back to the institution He had no means of earning money at present and he applied for a reduction of the order to 10s, the arrears to be paid off at the rate of Es 6d per week,—The Chief Constable remarked that the defendant had obtained a certificate of his sanity. The Bench deoided to adjourn the case for a month, and, In the meantime, they hoped the defendant would pay his wife at least 12s 6d per week and Es 61 arrears (15s in all). At the end of that period they would re-consider the amount to b paid. FREE Lormtsas.—Richard Hynes was charged with being drunk at the Police Station at 3 o'clock that morning.—P.C. Williamson said the prisoner Came into the charge office, in a very sirunken condition. looking for lo igings. He became so disorderly that witness was compelled to put hi-n in the cells.—Prisoner. who ha .1 a very black record, was fined 5s and costs with the option of seven days' imprisonment.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-02-08_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>APPOLLONIA CONCESRIONS, LIMITED.</head>
        <p>APPOLLONIA CONCESRIONS, LIMITED.</p>
        <p>At the statutory meeting of the AppoHoule Conces- sions, Limited, held on the 25th ult., the Chairrivin of 1 the Company took the opportunity of explaining the position and the prospects. He stated that within a month from the date of registration the whole of the documents, leases an papers on this side were all prepared and completed and their representative had left England for the West Coast to inspect the property. I The Chairman went on to say That no less than five I offers had been made for portions of the property and that negotiations were still being carried on. Ire rvly to a Shareholder, he stated that the. parties purchasing were not connected with this company and that the sales would be made with the Appollonit Concessions having to in any way put up money for the promotion of subsidiary companies,but that immediately the sales were complete, the cash and shares would be divided amon.st the Seareh-ild-rs of this Comnanv.</p>
      </div>
