      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-02-08_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <head>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS. Rugby Football.</head>
        <p>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS. Rugby Football.</p>
        <p>[By " Ovalist.l</p>
        <p>It seems a remarkably long tltne since we had th pleasure of watching a football match,and on tna t occa- sion the pleasure was limited. Last Saturday Oldham should have been Warringtons opponents, but the general mourning naturally caused a postponement, so that Warrington are now twl matches in arrear„ one with Swinton in the West Lancashire Cup Competition and the other just mentioned. If any nrogress is made in the Cupties Warrington should bs kept busy to the e id of the season.</p>
        <p>To-lay L.lgh should be at home to Warrington, but at the time of writiag it looks very imprObabl. that such an expo=edi ground as that at friativr-lane will be fit for p lay. J Frost, EA., has paid U 9 another visit,and as a rule his arranzemenrs do not fit in with those of football clubs. Usually one of the fiEures with Leigh is played off early on, but this seasoa the meetings between thee two well matched clubs have been loft until late. In recent years Leigh have had rather the best of matters on both grounds, and particularly at Warrington, but last season the locals splendidly avenged the defeat sus- tained at Wildersponl. I hope they will repeat the per performance this time and leave the lowly position now occupied in be league.</p>
        <p>The team which did so badly against Barrow has been chosen to oppose Leikh, and given decent weather they should acquit themselves with vastly more credit. Good football cannot be exoected from any team on such a day as that of a fortnight ago, and the Barrow match was no real test of tha abilities of either fifteen. It is quite time Warrington got into a winning mood, if only to raise our hopes for the cup ties.</p>
        <p>Oldham and Swinton have again failed to settle their differencea by a pitched battle. The contest should have come off on Wednesday, and with the rapid thaw one would have expected the ground to be alright, but un- fortunately it was not and thousands of peop'e were disappointed. fluodredi of them went to Oldham by train and:their rage can better be imagined than des- cribed at finding their journey fruitless. It doesn't speak very well for the Oldham F.C. management that the ground should be unplaysble after such fl atteriag repor:s as to its positive fitness.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-02-08_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <p>IF YOU FiA-NT-i.-lIRAL CHESHIRE GOOSE OR TURRET DON'T BE IMPOSED UPON WITH FOREIGN STUFF, SEE MC 11)ERIIOTT, THE RABBIT KING, NEAR BRIDGE FOOT, LATCDFORD,</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON.</p>
