      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-02-08_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>EXAMINER, FR! A 'V, FEBRUARY 8. 1901.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, FR! A 'V, FEBRUARY 8. 1901.</p>
        <p>NEWTON URBAN DISTRICT COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>Proclaiming the King.</p>
        <p>A meeting of this Council was held in the Town H4ll, Earlestown, on Monday evening. Mr J W Collingwood presided, and there were also present i.--Massrs C B F B)rron, C.C., E TEv Ins, J Fltz4erall, W Gilmqur, Dr Patterson, T Orman&amp; N S(ddelev,J Simpson, W Thomp- son, with A Cole (clerk), and A Bowee.(stirveYor.) THE N,EIIORIA.L SERVICE.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-02-08_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>CEIAIRMIN RECURNS TRANS.</head>
        <p>THE CEIAIRMIN RECURNS TRANS.</p>
        <p>The Chairman said that was th ) first opportunity he bad had since the impressive memorial service held on Saturday of expressing his thanks to the members of the Council and the inhabitants generally, for the manner in which every section of the community supported him in expressing their sympathy on the day of the Queen's funeral. He wished especially to thank the Salvation Army for the band, and the police for the way in which they assisted to keep order. He felt sure they would never forget the solemn occasion. (Hear, hear.) Ho was pleased to think that everything pissed off in such a satisfactory manner. (Hear, bear.) THE NEW BURIAL FEES. _ _</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the Cemetery Committee held on Mon- day last, the question of burial fee' was considered and the matter was referred to the Chairman of the Coinmittes to interview the clergy of the district and ascer- tain their views on the question of fees. Mr Gilmour complained that he had not received a copy of the new Burials Act. _ . .</p>
        <p>The Clerk stated that copies had been sent out. The Chairman said the omission to send Mr Gilm3ur one would be remedied at once. The minutes were passed. THE VOrE3 OF CONDOLENCE. .</p>
        <p>A letter vr-a-S-read. ir-o-m-Mrs-L-IOY-d:a-c-k-O-o-wledging the Council's kind expression of sympathy on the death of her husband. Mr Lloyd. Mr Joseph Swann wrote "I big to acknowledge the receipt of the very kind resolution passed on the death of my son Sam in South Africa and I wish you to convey to ttse Council the grateful thanks of Mrs. Swann and myself.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-02-08_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>GAS COMMITTEE.</head>
        <p>GAS COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of this committee the report of Mr. A. Bowes, the engineer pro tern. for the past five weeks, was read, from which it appeared that on the night of the 16th inst. the wooden covering over the liquor tank collapsed,the brick pillar in tho middle having given way It was resslved that the Chairman of this Committee he empowered to take such stnas with respect to the coll- apse of the covering over tee liquor tank as la may think necessary and that the questioa of obtaining additimal land for extending the Gas Works be re- ferred to the Extensions Sub-Committee with power to secure such land as thee may think neledsary. The Clerk read a letter which he had nceived from thi carters at the Gas Works app'ying for an increase of wages. It was resolved that the application be referred to the Extensions Sub-Committee, rho tender of Messrs James Smethurst and Son, to take the scrap iron lying at the Gas Works for the sum of £2 75 per ton was accepted. Mr Thompson proposed the adoption of the minutes ; Mr 0:m and seconded and they were carried. WATER COMIIIrTEE. The Chaim an rep irted to this committee that he had asked Mr Bowes, the Surveyor, to act as temporary water engineer since the death of Mr D H L'oyd, and his action was appreved.—The Clerk read letters which he had received from Mr J Bridge,of Parkside,Newton- le-Willows, stating that he had received a communica- tion from the London and North-Western Railway Co, stating that they intended to discontinue the supply of water from their main at Lowtoa Junction an and after the 31st instant, in accordance with the request of the Leigh Rural District Council. The Clerk also read his reply thereto, advising that arrangements should be made with the London and Nonh-Western Railway Company to continue the reapply for a few months. It was resolved that the matter be left to the Chairman of the Committee and the Clerk, and that the Clerk in— form Mr Bridge that the same is receiving the attention of the committee. The Chairman also read a letter which he had received from Mr George Sturges% of the Hand and Banner Inn, Newton-le-Wino we, with respect to his water account for the quarter ended at Christmas last. It was resolved that in cases where the accuracy of the water meters is disputed, consumers he allowed to have the meters tested by the Council, and that a charge of is be made hr the test should the Ewer prove to register correctly. The Clerk was instruct id to write to the secretary to the Local Goverment Hoard urging him to include, when granted, the Provisional Order in the first est of Bills to be submitted to Per iament for confirma- tion. Mr Borron moved the confirmation of the minutes, which were approved.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-02-08_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>HIGHWAYS AND SANITARY COMMITTEE.</head>
        <p>HIGHWAYS AND SANITARY COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>The Surveyor reported that as it was imaerative that new wheels should be obtained for the road roller,he had asked all the mak MS of road rollers to tender for the supply of the same, but that the maker eof the roller (Hessler Aveling and P orterl alone quoted for the supply of the four wheels, the amount ot toeir tender being £BO. I: was resolved that the tender of Messrs Avaling and Porter be accepted. Also that they be requested to state the amount which thoy will allow for the old wheels. The followiez tenders were also accepted, viz .—That of Mr G Finning, to provide and fix wooden gates and posts in Victoria-road for the sum of £5 per gate with posts ; that of Mr Peter Bredshaw to supply two wooden shelters for the sum of £l3. The following plans of new buildings were approved, viz. :—Messrs. Greenall. Whitley and Co., Limited, St Helens, public, house, Wargrave Old-read ; Earlestnwn Co-operative Society, Eerlestown ten cottages Heald-street ; E Martin 1, Grant-street, stove shed aril stable, both with corru- gated iron roofs and walls, Grafton-street. The follow- ing building plan was disapproved viz :—Earlestown Co-operative Society, Earlescown, shop and dwelling house at corner of Healdestreet and Earle-street The Surveyor submitted to the Committee his estirn ates of the expenditure on main roads for the year ending 31st March, 1902, It was resolved that the Council be recommended to apply to the Lancashire Count? Council for the sum of £778 104 for ordinary repair and mails- tenance, and £452 its 101 for special work. The Clerk reported that the National Telephone Company. Limited, were unable to comply with the request of the Council to connect the water works with the Telephone Exchange, line, unle s the Couecil consented to the erection of telephone poles in Seuthworth-ruad, and that ,he had been instructed by the Chairman of the C ommittee to grant the required pr. mission on Condition that the poles were placed in situations to be fixed by the Surveyor. The C'erk read a letter which he had received from Mr. A. C. Leslie (Agent to the Right Ron. Lord N owtnn). objecting to the placing by the Cnuneil on Lord New- ton's land of iron posts on which the worth" Public Footpath" appear. It was resolved that the surveyor be directed to remove from Lord Newton's land the posts in question, and to re-erect them on the highway or on oth'r sites over which Lord Newton has no rights. The posts on the common to which Lord Newton objrcted not to be disturbed, the Committee not reeognising any alleged rights of hi, Lordship thereto. Complaints hav- ing been received as to the dangerous state of the foot. path leading from Wargrave Road along the side of tlief Leaden and North-Western Railway too Pork-road, New- ton le-Willows, it was 'resolved that the Surveyor be directed to visit the lotus in quo and report to the Committee as to the whole question. The Clerk read a letter which be bad received from the Secretary to the E arleetown Co-operative Society, stating that as the Society were about to erect premises in School-street, it wonld be necessary for the Council to order the removal of their fence, telephone pole, &amp;c., which obstruct the entrance to the street. It Was resolved that the Sm. vevor be directed to prepare and submit t the Com- mittee a plan showing the alterations which it will be necessary to make to the entrance to the highway-yard, The Clerk submitted to the Committee the Tenders which be had obtained for the supply of hay and straw, to be delivered at the stab'ee in Crow-lans, during the year ending December 31st, 1901. Resolved that the ten- der of Mr William Longton, for the supply of bay at £4 per ton and of straw at £1 164 81 per ton he accepted. The Clerk submitted to the Cemmittee a circu- lar letter received from the Secretary of the Royal Com- mission on Sewage Disposal as to experiments on the purification of sewage, and reported that the forms sent therewith had been handed to the Surveyor for report log the results of his experiments. The Clerk read a letter from Mr. A C Leslie (Agent to the Right lion Lord Newton) stating that the Council Could have land for the deposit of dry ashes and rubbish on Pritchard'e Farm, alongside Watery-lane, on the following terms (inter alia) the land to be feneed off and, if required, roadthe and the Conn- cil top leaayrednintg up o to thesmealam'ontha after the deposit in completed at the rate of £3 per annum, ItUc. the tenant to be paid for one year's crop for the land taken. It was resolved that the land be rented from Lord New ton for use as a tip on the re-mean;tdrojectto;adnitdionass set forth in the memo then submitted to the commltee. The surveyor was directed to prepare and submit to house ther,,fu, e d committee:srurar roerpn rt destructorsup o nth feorptrhoivsisidoi to suitahl rhPrefer. It was resnivad that the warp of Joseph flindley (road f ireman) be Increased from 4.01 to 504 per week. The Chairman proposed the adoption of the minutes and they Were approved. THE PROPOSED TRXMWAYS.</p>
        <p>• AP meeting of the Tracuways flomraittPe, The Clerk read correspondence with Mesa'. F. J. Leslie</p>
        <p>and Co„the solicitors to the South Lancashire Train ways Cornpany, with respect to the wideaing of Mill-lane and other matters arising out of the propo.ed Bill. Be also read copy of the lstt er which he had addressed to the Chairman of the County Council Main R ads and Bridges Committee as t the widening of rown End Bridge and the reply of the County bridge master thereto, in which he stated that the bri it!, would Come under the opera- _ _ _</p>
        <p>tion of the agreement b .tweea the County Council and the promoters. It was resolved that the Clerk and the Surveyor be instructed to see Messes Leslie and.Co ,with respect toe—Tne widening Of Mill-lane from the London and North Western railway arches to the entrance to Newton Park so as eo allow a distance of not less than ein. between the nearest trans line and the outside of thelootpath ; the la4ing down of a single line in lieu of a double line shown on the deposited plans under the London and North Western Railway Arch, provided the Bard of Trade c oasent thereto ' • and in tnioevent of such content being refived the Compeny to agree to level up the line on the adjoining westerly arch so as to form an extra additioo to the road; and the widening of "rown Eld brides; et a further meeting the Clerk reported upon the interview which the Surveyor and he ' had had with Messrs. F J Leslie and Co., the solioitors to the South Lancashire Team wave C Ltd., an I read a letter which be had received from them agreeing to carry out the requirements of the Connoil, and to insert clauses in the proposed. Bill for the protection of the Cluncil on conditien Moot the Council did not oppose the Bill ; but, with respect to the widening of Mill-lane, Newton-le-Willows, Messrs. Leslie and Co., stated that the C erip any would make the required widening pro- vided that the land could be bought at a reasonable price. It was resolved thst the Clerk be iostruoted Co consult Messrs. Peace and Ellis. solicitors, with respect. to the proposed bill. The Clerk repertei to the Council on Monday that the engineer to the South Lancashire Tramways Ccmpany had forwarded drawings showing the construction of</p>
        <p>the tram lines. Tae Surveyor said the plans were highly satisfactory. Th 9 plane wsrn referred back to the Tramways Special Committee. . • THE WATER WORKS LOAN. The Cnairrnan move i me. this Couneil do rescind so much of the resolution passed on the sth day of Novomosr last (Minute N 3810, whereby the Council decided to apply to the Local Government Board for a Provisional Order to amend Section 53 of the Newton District Improvement Act by altering toe section so as to enable the Council to borrow for Water Works Pur- poses. subj tat to the sanction of the Local Government Board, the sum of £31,000 i. lieu of the sum of £6,000 authorised to 100 borrowed by Section 53 of the said Act, as relates to the amount to be borrowed, and that apdi- C%tion be made to the Local Government Board for a Provisional Order to Enable the Council to borrow such amounts as may be required from time to time Ine Water Works purposes. Mr. Ormand seconded and the resolution was carried.</p>
      </div>
