      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-02-08_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>INC RE 13E OP'73IL &amp;RIES.</head>
        <p>INC RE 13E OP'73IL &amp;RIES.</p>
        <p>_ The Finance Committee resolved that the loll:oaring I report of the Offiee Staff Committee be reedited 'and adopted, and that the in teas, in the salary of r Bowes (surveyor) take effect as and from the lit January instant :—that the Finance Committee be recommended: —(a) ro advertise far a gas and water engineer at a commencing salary of £l5O, rising to £3OO per annum, in the place of the late engineer; (b) to increase the salary of the Surveyor ('sir. Bowes) from £2OO to £250 per annum, on condition that he be held responsible for ithe laving of gas and water mains in all roads and streets lin the district, and for the repairs thereto; also that the I surveyor be appointed the toned of the sanitary depart- ' moat, and that the inspector of nuisances be under his direct iupervision ; (c) TJ grant the application of the surveyor for a youth to assist him in his work, and that he be Panto owered to engage a youth at a salary of £2O</p>
        <p>pr annum. The minutes wore adopt on the proolsition of Mr. Ortnlni. TEI_E EI:OTION OF URBAN court.ClLLoas.</p>
        <p>The Clerk reported that he had received a ,letter from the Local Government Board sitericg the date of toe election of Urban District C eancillora frai thn 14t &amp;Pril to the 25. h March. The alteration was die to the census being fixed for the first April. THE KING PROCLAIMED AT EARLESTOWN. The Plains:ma reported to the Council that it had been decided that he sliould publicly read the proclamation of the King. It had been done in varioes other districts similarly situated, and he thought the present was a fitting opportunity of doing lik .wise. It had not seemed to him an opportune time to proclaim it before the Funeral of thelr late Sovereign, but the present seemed a fitting opportunity. Promp7ly at 5 30 the winners of the Council left the Beard Room sad proceeded to take up a position on the Town Hall steps. A small gathering inemsdiately assembled notwithstanding the heavy fall of snow. Amongst those present including toe members of the Clunci, was the Rev T R Niatthews. The Onairman having ohtainsai a bicycle lamp prl. needed to read the proclarnat‘on in a loud voice. At the close the chairman led in the singing of the National Anthem in which all ore j Tee interesting ceremony concluded win the raising of cheers for his Majesty. On again proceeding to the 'Yard Rolm, the chairmen_amOved _.the following resolution "That a humble address be presented to His Msjesty King E I ward VII. setting forth that the Urban Disttict Council of Nowtonsin-Makerfield both for themselves and the inhabitants of th district respectfully beg to tender to his Majesty an assurance of their unswerving loyalty and devotion and a further assurance of their fervent hope that Ice may be blessed with health and strength to discharge the arduous dutiee of the high station to which he has heel called for tile beet° wal of whicn and all other good gifts upon his Majesty ml the Royal Family they will ever prays" Mr Orrnand said he had great pleasure in seconding</p>
        <p>the resolution. It Was carried unanimously.</p>
