      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-02-08_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>LADIES' LETTER.</head>
        <p>LADIES' LETTER.</p>
        <p>MY DEAR NELLIE,</p>
        <p>Many ties of soft black chiffon, with guipure ends, or successive rows of inch-wide and much narrower ribbons, are attached to wide collar- bands and stocks, and many sailor ties, knotted so that they appear some inches below the collar- band in the centre of the front as they have been " worn throughout the winter, are soft and pretty wear on blouses and on ordinary bodices. Black blouses, high or square cat, and a long range of black glace blouses, with corded tucks, are, per- haps, the quickest and most convenient mode of meeting the requiremer ts of the moment. Some .very pretty ones have wide cape collars of chiffon, bordered with silk guipure or is succession of flat tucks of chiffon., Petticoats of Week glace and soft black Materials have been deftly made, so that by a succession of flounces at the hem they make the skirt of the gown stand out well. These are somewhat new in their arrangement, and so are the toques formed entirely of black velvets, and the girdles made of ribbon with the long ends and loops in front tagged with jet.</p>
        <p>It may safely he said that the bolero will lose none of its popularity during the spring; in its many and varied lot Ins it proves too becoming to lightly cast aside ; tlicise banded round the waist, and with straight, stole-shaped pieces in front, however, seem the latest and most novel kind. On all these short coats, bolerost and Eton jackets, long loops and ends of broad ribbon are displayed, left to hang loose from the centre of the front; and reaching almost to the bottom of the skirt. A quiet type of gown is of black face-cloth with a tucked skirt, and a bolero faced with black glace, covered with cord braiding and put into a deep belt of the same, the eollarette, toque and muff being of black fox fur. Long tunics almost entirely concealing the under-skirts are seen on many of the newest gowns, and in plain cloth edged with fur they look particularly well, al- though they are rather heavy and cumbrous for walkingin. A pretty cloth gown is made in this way: The tunic is edged with mink, as in the short, rounded bolero; under which is a second (simulated) bolero, closely striped with narrow gold braid. The vest is of corded blue glace, and the belt of the same.</p>
        <p>Striped and all black fancy materials are suit- able to present events, and lend themselves to the new cut of skirts, and to the stitched bands and perpendicularly-pleated skirts now worn. Dull black ribbons, as wide sashes and stole ends, give a mourning aspect to an ordinary black dress and the trimming of gowns carried out in the fabric itself would seem to have the preference especially, numerous rows of cordings heading shaped flounces, and ia cloth the tacks that are simply one fold of the material with the edges left raw. Military btaidings and rich guipures made of decoupe cloth laid over glac6 silk are dressy, and well suited to the formation of tunics, and those who go in for simplicity will choose the soft falling woollens with the shaped flounces, and the full bodices with a distinct centre to the front, met by scolloped sides, which give length to the figure, and should bn accompanied by high collar-bands. These as often as not have chevron ends, which meet in the middle, and are placed over an underhand, a treatment that admits of silk being mid on cloth after the manner of mourning.</p>
        <p>• -no of the most fashionable women have adtpted: the black velvet bracelet, which the belles of I le- mid-Victorian eta used to wear so coquet- tishly but: the up-to-date bracelet is a more pie ee t affair, having loops and ands edged with or pearl ferrets, hanging from the left w• i-I, hilta bizarre ornameb clasps the band s•• ; ot,,e found the arm. Nothing shows off a • ::.,11t1 better than black velvet, and the same In„N, 1,0 -aid of the black velvet neckband, which is so in favour, studded with jewelled ornaments Or InL nouveau flowers. Many wear some form of a 1-end round the throat, usually a rucked piece of, tulle or velvet the same shade as the dress. Neck ruffles of silk edged with chenille, -and of 0014,,, bordered with black fur, are being ordered, as well as the long black fox fur boas and tippets, which is the deepest form of mourning in fur, and some of those who do not affect crape are trim. • ming the black cloths with narrow bands of • cata,,ul and broadtail. Three-quarter paletots sacquo•back coats of broadtail, and the long coats of the same reaching to the ground, are among the luxurious garinents of the moment, the most fashionable furs being these that are well adaptea for mourning. • Your loving, • Jowls.</p>
        <p>To tdovsnone.-IWri Winetow's Soothfrig'Nyruu has been used for over fifty years by millions of mothers for their children while teething, with perfect lareis. It will relieve the roorsufberet imrnediate:y It Is nlearant to taste ; end produces natt.rai oniet au,ep, oy reticvmg the child from pain nil the ittie uherub awakes "at b^ight ns n button." Of all Chemists le per bottle. Advt. TRUSS.—New washable waterproof is the most suitable for tropical climates • A is light, clean, cool, comfortable. and durable; can be worn whilst bsthing. WHITS WRIGHT, Surgical Instrument Makers, 93 Reushsw-street, opposite top of Bold-Areet, Liverpool. Advt.</p>
