      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-02-08_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>CHRISTOPHER TH ISTLETO (From a Photograph.)</head>
        <p>CHRISTOPHER TH ISTLETO (From a Photograph.)</p>
        <p>One of the most peculiar thirgs about the con- sumptive is that he will never admit that he has consumption until in the last stages of the disease, when there Is little hope of his recovery. This hopefulness is a rood thing,because it stimulates the desire for tieatment, but the saddest phase is that many persons who have a tendency to consumption do not begin the right treatment until it is too late. Let us call your attention to a letter receive 1 frem a gentleman whose condition was indeed most seri- ous :--</p>
        <p>"Crosby, Doncaster, "February. 13th, 1900..</p>
        <p>"Dear Sirs,—lt gives me very rnue-h pleasure tu let you know the great benefit I have derived from taking your preparaticn—Scott's Emulsion. I had been ill for twelve months, suffering from pleurisy and a weak chest, and dining that time I had frequently to discontinue my employment. I was unaer treatment for erme time but did not derive any permanent benefit, and finally became so low and weak that I could scarcely get about, and was . _</p>
        <p>told I bad consumption. On-a -day a frie-nd_ told me</p>
        <p>I ought to try Bcoti's Emulaion,alke knew it would do me good, and I am thankful to say I took his ad.</p>
        <p>vice: I soon found the preparation as doing me</p>
        <p>good ; my strength gradually returned, and at the end of two months' treatment I felt I was a new man ; and now at the end of six months I am ' thankful to Bay I am in good health, ar d havP not had to lose a day's work. I shall not tail to rr nom. int nd Scotts Emulsion, as I have found it is a grand thing for tuilding up the Fyitcm, aryl it entinot t e too widely known.—Believe me, Yours gratPfully, " (Signed) CHRISTOPIIER TUISTLETON."</p>
        <p>Itt the C 1113.8 towhich we have called your atter-</p>
        <p>tion, the gentleman took a staudard remedy which. has been auccessful in overcoming the early stages - of consumpticn for a quarter of a century. The result of his treatment he tsils in his own rangtmge, and we cannot refrain from emphasising the fact that he took Scott's Emulsion before it was too late. There is no doubt that many persona are torn with this weak tendency and struggle with it all through their lives, for the simple reason that they never realise the importance of vitality, or know the way to acquire this resistive force of life. The only way in the world that persons having a tendency to consumption earl overcome this tend- ency is to enrich the blood with the sUbSI eines that increase vi'ality. and thus enable Nature to permanently establish health in the place of weakness. Scott's Emulsion is the world-renowned Norway cod liver-oil rendered perfectly palatable and easy of dieestion• In no other form can this oil be taken with such great benefit to the system, and it 1.., therefore necessary to purchase only the genuine Scott's Emulsion. The plain cod-liver oil unnecessarily taxes digestion and is not BO readily ats-rbed as SCOWN Emulsion. Scott's Emulsion eases the coughing of a conew22P- tire, and it giyei to the entire system that rich blood without which health cannot hope to be main- tained. I gives force to the vital organs, strength to tt* lungs and nverermes all the wasting tenderces. It is a specific for all throat and lung affections. You can obtain a sample of Scott's Emulsion by sending threepence to cover postage to Scott and Sowne. Limited. 95, Great Saffron Hill, London, E.C., and mentioning the name of this paper.</p>
