      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-02-08_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>PLEASANT PARAGRAPHS.</head>
        <p>PLEASANT PARAGRAPHS.</p>
        <p>SOMETHING new in bonnets—Brains. MOST men are open to conviction until they go Into the defendants dock. HE "What is this, dear?" She : "Pound- 3ake." He: "It's rather small for its weight." WISDOM is greater than wealth, if it happens to be our wisdom and other people's wealth. POPULARITY consists as much of what you don't say as what you do. PREHISTORIO.—WiIIie "Father, what do they make talking machines of?" His Parent: "The first one was made out of a rib, my son." "Mx husband is very hard to please." "He must have changed considerably since his mar- riage." GRIMES (dejectedly): "What a perfect fool I am." Mrs. Grimes (soothingly): "No one is per- fect, dear." "MY grandparents married in haste." "And did they repent at leisure?" "Oh, yes; both lived to be over 90." TEACHER : "What should be done to a little boy who plays truant ? " Johnny (the truant, promptly ): "Keep him out of school, mum." PUPIL TEACHER : "Now, Tommy, tell us what a plagiarist is?" Tommy (promptly): "A man who writes plays." "I HAD a narrow escape in my house the other night." "How so?" "My wife shot at some burglars." " Ou, you wicked boy; how could you rob that nest? No doubt the poor mother is now grieving for the loss of her eggs." Urchin: "Oh, she doesn't care. She's up in your hat." "WHAT is firmness, father?" "Firmness my boy, is obstinacy in ourselves. "And what is obstinancy ? " "Obstinacy is firmness in some- body else." BROTHERLY Lovn.—" True friendship a myth? No, indeed. Why, I know a couple of men, no relation to each other, and yet they are like brothers." "What are they fighting about?" "WHY is it, will you please tell me, that some -women look so ugly on the bicycle ? " "Because they are the same women who look so ugly off the bicycle." THE Minister's Wife: "Why, here is a safety pin in the collection." The Minister: "Yes, I fancy the man who used to pub in a pants-button is now married." TEACHER: "But can't you define bicycle ' ? Suppose someone asked you what a bicycle is, what would you say?" Pupil: "I'd say, Don't you know what a bicycle is? ' " MRS. TOP (reprovingly) : "Amelia, where do you suppose you will go if you tell such false- hoods ?' Amelia: "I am sure I don't know and don't care. I've got friends in either place." A MATTER OF FINANCES.—Bachelor "I don't believe in long engagements."—Benedict "Nor I, if one wishes to have enough money left to set up housekeeping respectably." AT THE Zoo.—Tommy "Is that a he or a she ' lion, papa? Pater: "Which one, dear?" Tommy: "That one with his face scratched and the hair off the top of its head." Pater (with a sigh): "That must be a male, my son." "HAVE You ?"—lt is well known that some of the novels bearing Dumas's signature are not his own. Meeting his son one day, the author of "Monte Cristo" asked him whether he had read his latest novel. "No," said Dumas, junior, who was remarkable for his ready wit. "Have you ?" THE governess was giving little Tommy a grammar lesson the other day. "An abstract noun," she said, "is the name of something which you can think of, but not touch. Can you give me an example?" Tommy: "A red-hot poker." SHE UNDERSTOOD.—Mrs. Muggins : "And so y_9u are an actor? What are you playing now?" Handsome Stranger: "The dual role in the 'Corsican Brothers.'" Mr. Muggins "Oh, yes, I've Been that, and I remember there was a duel in it." IT TOLD A TALE.—Husband: "How pretty you look I"—Wife : "Yes • I notice that as often as the style changes you find my old dresses most charming." THE SAME KIND.—It is stated that a ham sandwich has been dug out of the ruins of Pompeii in a perfect state of preservation. When tasted it Was thought to be very similar to those to be got at the railway refreshment-rooms. PHILOSOPHY.—The philosopher laughed aloud. "A million I" he exclaimed. "Why, such wealth Would make me insane 1" For he, understand, had relatives. WHY THE MILK WAS WARM.—" What makes the milk so warm?" said Betty to the milkwoman, When she brought the pails to the door one morn- ing. "Pleese, mum, the pump handle broke, and inissns took the water from the biler." BEGAN Wsoara.—" Bride. "I'm so afraid peo- ple will find out that we're just married that I've made Will promise to treat me in public just as if he had no thought for any one but himself." Matron: "I adopted that plan when I was married, trz,d my husband never got over it." THE W ?.YS OF A RULER.—" Are you sure he is s real duke ?" asked the business partner. "He must me, he's so proud," said the possible father- in-law. "He threatened to have me kicked out of the hotel when I said something about having the money settled in Milhe's name." A FARMER'S DAY.—" I suppose," said the farm hand, who was looking for a job, "that you be- lieve ;n the eight-hour day ?" "That I do," said the fa:mer. "I do eight hours in the foreneon and eight in the afternoon, but along about hay and harvest time I put in two or three hours extra." BREAKING IT.—An accident had occurred in a quarry, and a man had been killed. An Irishman volunteered to break the news gently to les wife, Which he did in the following manner :—Fat "That was a sad accident to your husband's gold Watch, Mrs. Murphy."—Mrs. Murphy: "Shure, an' it's nivver a watch he had, now."—Pat: "Be my soul, and that's lucky 1 for there's ten ton o' rock just fallen on him ?' "AND so you have brought my beautiful poodle home, have you, like an honest man, instead of keeping him yourself, as you might easily have done?" said the delighted lady, as she fondled the poodle. "Were you not strongly tempted to keep the darling creature?" "No, mum," replied the man, as he pocketed the twenty shillings reward. "It weren't no temptation. I couldn't have sold his hide for threepence at this season of the year, mum." ANOTHER VERSION. A new definition of a gentleman was given recently by the plaintiff in an action brought in a county court. The defendant Was described in the different sets of papers as a butcher, a builder' and a tailor. The judge finally appealed to the plaintiff, who replied: "Well, he is more like a gentleman than anything else. He walks about all day doing nothing, going from public, house to public-house." BLUNDERED AGAIN.—" going to give up being a society idiot," said the bashful man, "and confine myself to talking sense." " Done it again ?" asked his unfeeling friend. "I should say I have. I wanted to tell Mrs. Bloom that she must have married very young to have a daughter as old as hers, and got it twisted into telling her that she must have been married an awfully long time ago." ONE o His HABITS.—Miss Awfulface "Old Frosty refused Percy Hansomeface the hand of his daughter Evangeline, so Percy planned an elope- ment." Miss Sassyface "Did they really elope?" Miss Awfulface "Yes. Last night Percy approached the house cautiously and placed a ladder at Evangeline's window, and she escaped While her father slept soundly in the very next room." Miss Sassyface "How exciting! Well, Percy knew how to steal a march, didn't he?" Miss Awfulface " Yes ; he was a comic-opera composer." A certain curate was of a painfully nervous tem- perament, and, in consequence, was constantly tnaking awkward remarks—intended as compli- ments to tne bishop and others. Having distin- guished himself in an unusual degree during a gathering of the clergy to an afternoon tea at the bishop's palace, he was taken to task for his fail- ings by a senior curate, who was one of his com- panions on the way home. "Look here, Bruce," said the senior decidedly, "you are a donkey ' Why cannot you keep quiet instead of making Four asinine remarks? I am speaking to you now as a brother—" Loud laughter interrupted hire at this point, and or the moment he wondereC Why.</p>
      </div>
