      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-02-08_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</head>
        <p>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</p>
        <p>St. Paul's Church—Annual Tea Meeting.</p>
        <p>On Monday evening the annual tea party, dramatic entertainment, concert and dance, in connection with this place of worship, took place in the Parr Hall. No fewer than 800 persons eat down to tea and this num- ber was considerably augmented afterward. At eight the tables were removed from tha body of the ball, and several speeches with reference to the past year's work were delivered. Alderman Sutton presided. and he was supported en the platform by the Vicar (the Rev E R Tarbuck), Mr Robson John Chorley (vicar's warden), Mr J H Smethurst (people's warden), Mr A H Burgess (bon. stcretary), and Mr Lawton (one of the sidemen).— The Chairman, who was warmly applauded, said that St. Paul's parish was probably in a more flourish- ing condition than it had previously been, and he thought they would all agree with him when he said that that was due mainly to the exertions of the Vicar and his good wife—(applause)—who, ever since their arrival in Warrington, had done all they could for the welfare of the church and every member of the congre- gation. (Renewed applause). Praise was also bestowed upon the wardens.—Mr. J. H. Smethurst nsade a finan- cial statement of a very satisfactory tharacter. They were, as a church, never in a more prosperous financial condition than now, and the collections increased Sunday after Sunday. It was pleasing to be able to say that up to the previous day—from Easter Sunday of last year—the collections had totalled up to the handsome sum of £316 7s 4d, as against £291 8s 7d, the previous year, or an increase of £25. Of the sum of £316, £lO7 108 7d had been devoted to outside purposes of a most worthy character, such as the Indian Famine Fund and the hospital. The hospital collection furnished a record for the last 20 years, and they also had the satis- faction of sending a record amount to the Waifs and Strays Society a fortnight ago. (Applause). Reference was made to the Endowment Fund, and the speaker expressed his opinion that the congregation could best show their appreciation of the work of the Vicar and Mrs. Tarbuck by continuing to subscribe liberally in that direction. Theirs was the hest lighted church in the town, owing to the fact that they used the incan- descent light throughout the building, and in this res- pect thanks were due to Mr. Chantey, who ! looked after the work and showed great interest in the parish in a variety of other ways (Applause.)— The Vicar also spoke, and announced that a munificent gift had been made to the parish in the shape of about three-quarters of a statute acre of land for the purpose of the provision of a bowling green and parochial hall. It was an exceedingly valuable gift, and the site was not snore than a couple of stones throw from the church Although at present they bad only the land, they meant to get the bricks and mortar to make the land as profit- able as possible to the church. The donor of the site was the Hon Mrs Charles Harbord, of Pont-street, Lon— don (grand-daughter of the late Lord Winmarleigh),whe bed been kind enough to give them the land, through, no doubt,the intercession of Mr John White. (Applause.) The speaker concluded by proposing a very comprehen- sive vote of thanks to the chairman, subscribers, secre- tary, committee, lady helpers, stewards, ticket sellers and the musical and other performers.—This was secon-1 ded by Mr Chorley, and carried unanimously.—Alder- man Sutton briefly returned thanks.—A farce in one act, entitled "An April Fool," was then enacted. It was of a most amusing character, and was exceedingly well played. The characters were as follows :—Mr Oldbuck (a fine old Englislegentleman), Mr R G S Finney ; Mr Sparks(a fast young man), Mr H Houghton ; Mr Ped- dles (a mild young man), Mr A G Robertson ; Muffins (a charity boy). Master S Partridge ; Diana Oldbuck (daughter of Oldbuck), Miss Cissie Chesworth ; Lady Toxopholites, the Misses Houghton, A Smethurst, A and J Wainwright. —An ad- sourranent was now made to the (-Small hall, were the first of two concerts was given. An excellent pro- gramme, was gone through by the following ladies and gentlemen :.—Mrs W B Roberts, the Misses L Wenham and Dorothy Truelove, and Messrs, Clifford, Knowles, and A Fieldhouse. Later on another concert was given and in the same place, the artistes being St. Paul's choir, the Misses Wilcox and Bate, and Messrs. J Handley, Radcliffe, Emson, Geldard, Richardson, Smith. and Scattergood. A programme of dance music was gene through in the large hall. Ur. Walter Flood was the accompanist, and the music was supplied by Mr. Andeiton's band.</p>
