      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-02-08_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>LICENSING MATTERS. Applications to the Warrington Magistrates.</head>
        <p>LICENSING MATTERS. Applications to the Warrington Magistrates.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington Police Court, on Monday, before Mr. F. Monks and other magistrates, Mr Browne applied on behalf of Messrs Greenall, Whitley and Co., for per- mission to make a structural alteration in the upstairs portion of the Pelican Inn, Buttermarket-street. Be said the application bad nothing to do with the drink- ing area, but at the Pelican seven different lodges of Oddfsllows met, and one of the lodges had several hundred members, and they could not get into the room. The application had been approved by the Watch Committee, and it was supported by the Chief Constable. The Chairman: But drink is supplied in the room ? Mr. Browne: At suppers and dinners. The Chief Constable : It is used for the ordinary purposes of a clubroom. I have carefully examined the premises, and I think the alterations would be a desirable improvement. I have reported to that effect to the Watch Committee, who unanimously approved of the proposed alteration subject to the sanction of the Bench.</p>
        <p>The application was granted. Mr Browne then made an application with respect to the White Bull Inn, Horsemarket-street. He said the tenancy was a new one and it was found that the house accommodation was very inadequate. There was a large billiard room running alongside Town Hill, and it was proposed to cut off half of the billiard room, make it into a kitchen, remove the old kitchen, and provide lavatory accommodation. The Chief Constable said he was astonished to find what insufficient residential accommodation had been provided for the tenants. What was proposed was a fair solution of the difficulty. It reduced the drinking area of the house.</p>
        <p>This application was also granted. Mr. W. D. Jeans, jun., applied for permission, on behalf of Messrs Walker and Son to make certain altera- tions to a house at the corner of Cockhedge-lane and James street. It was prpposed to set back an off-licensed house, to give a strip of land to the Corporation for widening the street, and to take in a house at the rear. The Chief Constable.—lt simply sets the street line back. This alteration has also been approved by the Watch Committee. Mr Jelns.—The sanitary accommodation is very deficient. The alteration will make more room for the tenant.</p>
        <p>The Chairman.—Ought not boners like this to be con- demned altogether. Mr Jeans.—lt is practically being rebuilt. Mr Thornton.—There is a public-house close to. The application was granted. The following transfers of licences were granted From W W Whittle to Peers Brimelow of the Ring-o'- Bells, Church-street ; from Thomas Edward Brumbell, of the White Bull, to John Sefton ; from the late Mrs Kiernan, Patten Arms Hotel, to Miss Mary Emily Kier- nan; and from Thomas Arthur Sutton, of the Saracen's Head Hotel, Wilderepool, to Robert Sbacklady.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-02-08_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>An Army Pensioner's Death—Thirty-six Years in the Army. Mr. S Brighouse, County Coroner, conducted an inquiry at the Police Station on Alouday evening, into the circumstances attending the death of an army pensioner named William Joseph Byrne, 64 years of age, who resided at 54, Battersby-lane.— From the evidence of the deceased's daughter, Ida Marian Byrne. it appeared for some time the de- ceased had suffered from rheumatism and bad had to go upstairs on his hands and knees. At 10 30 on Wednesday. January 30th, he left the kitchen to go upstairs. When ball way up, he slipped and fell to the bottom. Witness went to his assistance, and seeing that be waQ severely injured went for Dr, Peacocke. The doctor found her father was suffer- ing from concussion of the brain. He died at 9.30 On Saturday morning, never regaining consciousness.— The jury returned a verdict of " Accidental death.' —The deceased had been connected with the army, for thirty-six years. He enlisted in the Scots Guards in 1864 and remained in that regiment until 1868, when he joined the newly formed 2nd Battalion 14th Foot Regiment as Sergeant-Major. In 1875 he entered the 3rd Kings Liverpool Regi- ment, and retired from the Army in 1890 owing to old age. lie bad been a Sbrgeant-Major for 22 years. Be leaves one son and four daughters, the son being on active service at the Cage as a petty officer in the navy.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-02-08_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>Personal.</head>
        <p>Personal.</p>
        <p>Out of over forty applicants, P.C. Pennington, of the Warrington Police Force, has been appointed relieving officer for the Prescot District of the Prescot Union. He has been connected 'with the force for six years,baving served in the Police Office during the greater portion of that period. He will commence his duties in his new position on Monday next.</p>
      </div>
