      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-02-08_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>(Copyright.)</head>
        <p>(Copyright.)</p>
        <p>THE ROSE OF PLYMOUTH.</p>
        <p>BY JUSTIN McCARTHY, Author of "Dear Lady Disdain," "The Dictator," "Maid of Athens," tsc.</p>
        <p>There may be prettier seaports than Plymouth, but I have not seen many more beautiful, although I have been about the round world a good deal in my time. Ido not say it is equal in loveliness to Nauplis in Greece, or to Quebec, or to Smyrna, or even to Naples; but I do say that in England it is not possible to light upon a more picturesque and delightful spot. Generally speaking, a seaport has no charm on its landward side. But the country around Plymouth is simply delightful in the artistic sense—a poet could not but be glad in such companionship. lam not a poet, but I know that if I were I could not help being glad in the com- panionship of sea and sky and shore at Plymouth. _ _</p>
        <p>Now, I had an adventure once, if that be not too grand a name to call it by, in which I bore rather a passive than what might be termed a thoroughly heroic and forefront sort of part, but which may, however, be worth narrating. I had been wandering with my family a good deal about Cornwall and Devonshire and the south-west of England generally, and we were greatly charmed with Plymouth, and made up our minds to have a little stay there. We had been recommended to a most delightful hotel, and we were lucky enough to find some friends in Plymouth. The friends were General and Mrs. Plymley, whom we had known: some years before in London literary society—General Plymley had written an account of the Indian Mutiny, and was rather proud of being thought an author; and Mrs. Plymley had written a volume of really pretty poems, and was not unambitious of being regarded as another Sappho. When we knew them first their only child, Rose Plymley, was but a little girl; now we found her a beautiful young woman. We had lost sight of them so long .--for General Plymley had been serving in various campaigns in countries to which our modest travels had not penetrated, while his wife and daughter had mostly been settled down in places like Bath or Bournemouth, to which we did not particularly want to go. Now, however, time was getting on, and I was no longer madly eager for foreign journeying, and so it happened that we went quietly to the West of England and came by mere adventure on our old friends again. My wife and I were struck with the beauty the grace, and the general charm of Rose Plymley. My wife at once christened her "The Rose of Plymouth," and I cordially accepted the descrip- tion and the phrase. The Plymley family had settled down to a residence in Plymouth—none of them cared much about London, and all of them loved sea and sky—and I soon learnt in an unexpected sort of way why Rose—the Rose. of Plymouth—cared so little for London society. It happened thus wise. We got into the habit of making excursions together every day, and wander- ing about the sea beach and the beautiful country. One day Mrs. Plymley could not come with our party, and, so quite naturally, my wife strolled along with General Plymley, while the charming Rose was left in my care. I could not help seeing that she seemed sad and worn out and spiritless, and one of my occupations is that of a writer of novels—and, engaged in such a business, lam naturally bound to take notice of the ways of young women. Who, I want to know, could write a novel if he did not make some studyof the ways of young women? "My dear girl," I said abruptly—you see I am, to put it mildly, an elderly gentleman, and might say "my dear girl "—"I see you are in some trouble; can you tell me anything about it? Could I help you? Could my wife help you? Be sure that either of us would do all we could. But don't say anything,if you would rather not." "I have read all your novels," she said hesitat- ingly, as if she feared she was saying too much— which she certainly was not, so far as I was con- cerned—"and I like all your heroines." I murmured my thanks for this kindly recogni- tion of the merits of my young women.</p>
        <p>"Yes," she went on dreamily. "I suppose you understand a great deal about young women ? " "Well, I have certainly tried to make them a study, but I do not know whether I have quite arrived at understanding them so far."</p>
        <p>"9h yes, von do urtmqd ttl‘jx,m Well, no./ lvTiant ibu to understand one, and to let like tell you my story and to give me your advice." "Nothing could be a greater delight to me," I said quite earnestly. "Toll me what your trouble is." . .</p>
        <p>"Well, to begin with—l am in love—with a young man." "Most young women who are worth anything are in that condition. But where does the trouble come in 7—and may I ask who is the young man? Of course, I take back the question if it ought to be a secret for the time."</p>
        <p>"Not from you," she said resolutely. "He is a great friend of yours—he looks up to you with intense admiration—Arthur Marradele." "Arthur Marradele—the young painter of sea- pieces? "Yes; I am in love with him." "And he is in love with you?" "Yes," she answered modestly. "Are you sur- prised ? " "Dearest girl, not the least in the world! I should only be surprised if, having met you, he were not. Had you and I met at a time—well, before you were born, I should have been in love with you too." "I am very fond of you, she said sweetly.</p>
        <p>- . "And I of you, although we did not hit off the world quite at the right time. But now I want to know where the trouble comes in. For if I had a daughter like you, I could wish her no happier fate than to be married to a young fellow like Arthur Alarradele. Why, there is genius in that young man, and he has a bright career before him —you may bet your bonnet-strings on that," I added fervently, relapsing into old familiar memories of American slang. "I am so glad to hear you speak like that," she said, turning, and even flashing, grateful eyes on me, "but my father will not listen to anything of the kind. He regards painters and artists of all sorts as mere Bohemians of the wildest kind; and even my mother, who is entirely on my side, cannot get him to change his ground." "Well, now, dear child, what can I do to help you ? " "That is exactly," she said pathetically, "what I want you to tell me. You see, you have invented all sorts of plots and pulled no end of your heroines out of their difficulties—now, can't you regard me as one of your heroines, and pull me through " and as she spoke she laid her hand affectionately on mine, and gave it a tender and loving pressure. Lives there the elderly gentleman who under such inducement would not have striven to come to ths rescue of a suffering damsel? So I thought it over in silence for a time, and at last I looked proudly up with the air of a</p>
        <p>scientific discoverer who has found something new, and I said triumphantly: "My dear girl, I have got it I" Her face flushed with joy and hope, and she said eagerly: "Do tell me what it is " I paused for a moment; the revelation was too stupendous for instant utterance; and then at last I brought it out. "Dear girl, make love to me!" "Make:lova to you ? " She drew back, rather in utter puzzlement than in consternation, for she</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-02-08_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>EXAMINER URIDAY, FEBRUARY 8 1901.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER URIDAY, FEBRUARY 8 1901.</p>
        <p>knew well that I had no thought but that of help- ing her out of her trouble. "Why, certainly ! " I used an American phrase again, from old association with America. "Why shouldn't you? Am Iso awfully old or so fear- fully ugly ? " "Oh, no; of course I don't say that!" she whispered, and she gave my hand a kindly touch. "You have read Sbakspere, Rose dear, and you remember how the lovely fairy Queen Titania fell in love with even a greater—"</p>
        <p>"Yea, yes; I know all about that," she broke in hurriedly, cutting me short in my sentence. I had meant to say: "With even a more evident ass than I am." But she went on: "I don't know how my making love to you would help matters or would me."save "Will you put yourself in my hands, a-ld takb my word I'll pull you through? You have said yourself that I have pulled my heroines through all sorts of difficulties. Then, as you say, just you become for the moment one of my heroines, and see if I can't pull you through."</p>
        <p>"But how about Arthur ? " she murmured tremulously.</p>
        <p>"How about Arthur? Why, of course, we are going to take him into the plot. You don't sup- pose he is likely to be jealous about a man of my time of life ? " "Oh, no!" she exclaimed fervently; and then, evidently on the second thought that she had been too frankly disparaging my charms, she hurried to add: "When we have let him into the secret. But then about your wife—might not she be annoyed?" "Why, of course, we'll take her into the plot too. She is very romantic at heart even still, and will be delighted to help you in your love affair— and, bless your heart, she will prove an excellent ' pal.' " "Your wife is a charming woman and a dear friend," she said, "and I could trust anything to her."</p>
        <p>"Nr.ery well; now leave it to her and to me, and to Arthur as well, to fix things. I think you had better begin a little of the love-making to-night. Just a little." "Ohl" she murmured, "but I don't know what I am to do—and your wife and Arthur are not in the secret yet."</p>
        <p>"Never mind about that; I can answer for my wife, and I am sure you can answer for Arthur."</p>
        <p>"Yes, I suppose so," she said lingeringly—"oh, yes, I am sure I can."</p>
        <p>"Dear girl, you surely do not suppose that ha would think you were likely to fall in love with me 7"</p>
        <p>"N —no," she answered diffidently, still under the influence of a kindly desire not to admit that she regarded me as a grizzled, grim old fogey, with whom no young woman could possibly fall in love. I fully understood the meaning of her intonation, and felt quite childishly grateful to her. "Well, now, we'll begin," I said encouragingly. "How shall we begin ? " she asked, in a tone of something like alarm. "Oh, well—we'll go on very gently until we have given the straight tip to our two fellow-conspirators. To-night we'll simply hang a little behind and let your father and my wife look out for us and wait for us a bit."</p>
        <p>"Oh, well—l don't mind that," she said, appar- ently much relieved. "Nor I," was my response, and I really did not mind it in the least. So we two hung behind and studied the trees and the shrubs, and went into more or less sincere raptures over every glimpse that we caught of the sea in the fading sunset. My wife and I were to dine with the Plymleys that night, and the time to dress for dinner was near at hand. Suddenly General Plymely "halted" ray wife, if I may put it so, and turned round towards us and called out.</p>
        <p>"Why don't you come along, you two ?" he asked, warmly, but not angrily. "We were looking at the stars," I answered in sweet, poetic tones. "Yes, we were looking at the stars," Rose repeated, in a softer and more tender voice. "Oh, hang the stars !" exclaimed General Plymley ; "the stars are always there at this time of evening, and you can stare at them as much as you like; but just now we have to get ready for dinner."</p>
        <p>"We must part," said Rose thrillingly. The girl was really acting very well.</p>
        <p>"Yes, we must part," I repeated. "Why, you are ping t2net dinner in less than half an hour I " the gallant Gene7tre:lTl -Wrtt Khasi&amp; would think you were a pair of young lovers!" "Alas! not a pair of young lovers," I murmured. "Well, let's go and get ready for dinner, any- how," General Plymley broke in. I saw that my wife's eyes were fixed wonderingly on me. But I knew well that, however she might be puzzled for the moment, she would never suspect that anything was going wrong. Before dinner was ready I had let her into the whole secret, and she declared that she was only too delighted to stand in and help poor Rose through her trouble. "It's such a new chance for me," she said, beamingly, "to play the part of the jealous wife. I never had the slightest opportunity before —not in all my married life."</p>
        <p>"My dearest," I interposed, "you are not alto- gether complimentary to me and to my personal attractions." "How is that?" she asked, with a manner of the sweetest simplicity.</p>
        <p>_ . "Well, because it rather suggests that no woman ever thought me worth looking after."</p>
        <p>_ "I thought you worth looking after," she said, sweetly, "and I think I did most of the love.</p>
        <p>making. But then I was always easily pleased, and I can't say that I saw any other woman trying to give me occasion for any pangs of jealousy. So that makes my new part all the more interesting to play, and I'll do my best to turn it to good account for my sweet little Rose of Plymouth."</p>
        <p>So that was easily arranged. We had a pleasant dinner. I had Mrs. Plymley beside me, and I amazed the poor woman by darting eye-glances every moment at Rose, who each time I gleamed upon her drooped her pretty head and lowered her eyelids as if in uncontrollable emotion. At every such interchange of looks my wife turned on us with glittering eyes a flashlight of impatient in- quiry. Poor Mrs. Plymley began evidently to think that something was going very wrong; but it was clear to Ina that she had not the slightest notion of what it was all about. I was amused and perhaps just the least little bit • in the world disconcerted at the thought, only made too certain by her manner, that she never fancied there could be anything in me to attract any young woman in the world. I was much enjoying the whole little comedy—but let me say, in justice to myself, that most of the enjoyment consisted in the belief that my wife and I were about to accomplish the happiness of the Rose of Plymouth and her lover. When the ladies had left the dining-room I sat in absolute silence. I smoked a cigar and drank a glass of claret, but could not be roused to say anything. When we went upstairs, I made for Rose at once, and she drew her skirts about her with that charming and expressive action which in- dicates that a woman wishes you to sit next to her, Then I asked her to take a turn on the laws with me. She professed a loud-spoken and abundant willingness, and we wandered out. There was a lovely lawn, on which the windows of the drawing-room—on the ground-floor in this dear old-fashioned house—directly opened. Rose gathered up her skirts, and we passed out together, my wife darting a perfectly flaming glance of wonder first after us and then at our host and hostess. Meanwhile Rose and I wandered far</p>
        <p>down the lawn, and I asked her in low tone: "Is he coming ? " "Oh, yes," she whispered. The truth is that Iler lover was staying and sketching at Newlyn, efrA I had wired to hun to come along in time to let Rose tell him all about things that night. He came, of course, and I left them to themselves. They rambled off together, and I hid myself far away among the trees. After a time that seemed to me hours, and to her and him no doubt only seconds, she found me out. "I suppose we had better go in," vile said. "Well, they will be looking out for wk, no doubt. Has he gone? and does he understand ? " "Oh, yes, it is all right," she answered grate- fully. Then I took her hand in mine, and thus audaciously we wandered towards the window of the drawing-room. General Plymley and my wife came out together, evidently looking for us, and we suddenly and somewhat theatrically tore our hands asunder.</p>
        <p>"Rose," said General Plyrnley, "it is rather late to be out on the grass." "My dear," asked my wife, with marble-like coldness, "don't you think it is quite time for us to be going back to the hotel—to say nothing of the chance of Rose's taking a chill in the night air? "</p>
        <p>"Yes," I answered submissively. Rose."</p>
        <p>"Farewell,</p>
        <p>"Why, we are all meeting at luncheon to- morrow!" my wife sharply struck in. "You are not parting with Rose for a lifetime!" "No," I replied sentimentally; "I am glad to remember that."</p>
        <p>As we were parting for the night I heard my wife say:</p>
        <p>"Dear G:nernt Plymley, don't you think it is about time that Rose should get married? "She is very young yet," he answered hesi- tatingly. "Very young—yes, but also very pretty and attractive, and not at all too young to turn the heads of men, and to disturb the happiness of households."</p>
        <p>"Oh, I hope Rose will never disturb the happi- ness of households," he said very gravely. "I hope she has not done so already," said my wife; and then I bade Rose good-night, and tor wife and I went off together—a little apart. "That was a good parting shot of yours," I said when we had got well out of range. "Yes—wasn't it ? " she asked in answer. "Do you know, dear, that I think between us both we have done the trick and made two young lovers happy." "Did I over-act my part to-day? " I asked meekly.</p>
        <p>"Oh, no, I don't think so. The part of an elderly married gentleman falling in love with a pretty young woman can't be over-acted." Then we looked into each other's eyes and laughed a merry laugh of perfect understanding. We walked home along the moonlit road, and talked over our plans. _</p>
        <p>"I think we must bring this thing to a head very quickly," my wife said, and she pressed my arm. "Yes, I think so too—only I don't quite see my way." "As if a man ever saw his way in any delicats condition of affairs!"</p>
        <p>"Well, do you see your way ? " "I shall try to look it out, and then I'll tell you all about it."</p>
        <p>I was satisfied and confident, and we said no more on the subject that night. My wife went to work very adroitly. She applied herself to Mrs. Flyrnley, and let her quite into the secret of the whole comedy. Mrs. Plymley had always, as I have said, been in favour of letting her daughter choose her own laver, but she had not known as much about that lover as my wife was able in perfect conscientiousness to tell her. Mrs. Plymley had liked him herself from what she had seen of him, but my wife was able to speak up as to his character, his artistic prospects, and his present position. Marradele was now staying in our hotel, and we talked over many a scheme for a coup d'etat. For we all thought that nothing short of a genuine coup d'etat could rise to the romantic dignity of the situation. I kept on meeting Rose, on the lawn and in the woods, morning, noon, and night. I generally slunk away after a while and allowed my place to be taken by Marradele, who was always lingering somewhere in the background.</p>
        <p>At last the well-ordered crisis came. One even- ing after dinner Rose and I were wandering on the tra and in the woods ag usual, and my wife made direct appeal to General Plymley. "Do you think it is right," she asked, "for Rose to be always philandering round alone with my husband? What is the meaning of it all ? " "Rose means no harm, I am sure," he said hastily. "Nobody means any harm in anything," m 9 wife replied somewhat sharply; "but this particular form of meaning no harm might well, I think, dis- please you. It certainly does displease me." "Dear lady," the gallant General said, "I'll speak to Rose—or rather, Mrs. Plymley shall speak to her." "I'll not speak to her!" Mrs. Plymley said firmly but benignantly. "She set her heart upon young Marradele, and everybody tells me he is a rising young man in his art, and of the very noblest character, and if you won't let the girl follow her heart's choice, I shall have nothing to say to her in remonstrance. lam deeply sorry if her goings-on give any pain to our dear friend here; but I am sure there is no harm meant on either side, and the girl must have something to give her affection to." "See 1 " my wife suddenly said—"there they are—walking on the lawn still! They are like a pair of engaged lovers General Plymley, I can't stand it any longer I " The night was, fortunately for our purposes, very dark; no star to be seen in the sky—and all that General Plymley could make out was that two figures—one in pantaloons and one in petti- coats—were walking at the far extreme of the lawn and towards the belt of trees, and each holding the hand of the other.</p>
        <p>"Oh ! by Jove, I must put a stop to this ! " "Pray don't bo violent, dear," his wife pleaded. The General made no reply, hut rushed out on to the lawn with all the impulse of a stout old soldier.</p>
        <p>"Come indoors, Rose ! " he exclaimed. "Let us have no more of this sort of thing And 1 must say that I do wonder at you, who are supposed to be a good friend of mine, encouraging my silly daughter in such nonsense! And then he suddenly saw that the man walking with his daughter and who had been clasping her hand was not the narrator of this little story, but was simply Arthur Marradele. "Good Heavens!" the stout old General cried; "where is---" and he spoke out my name.</p>
        <p>"I am here all, right," I answered, emerging from among the trees. "You see, Plymley, I knew from old experience, as you have known also in your time, that two are company and three are none."</p>
        <p>The General was an able man, and after a puzzled moment or two his intelligence grasped the meaning of the whole situation. He saw that a comedy with a purpose had been played, and he realised with a certain sense of humour where his own fitting part ought to come in. He rose to the occasion.</p>
        <p>"1 see—l see " he said. "You have managed that well among you. I am the reconciled father. Bless you, my children," he added, with excellent imitation of the manner of the heavy father of ancient melodrama, and he took a hand of Rose's and a hand of Arthur's, and he put them together, and he kissed his daughter fondly. "You managed that well, Rose!" "-1;,,, dear," she said with some confusion;</p>
        <p>.•our two mends here --turning to me ana my wife—"managed it for me." "Well," he said joyously, "let us come into the house and drink a glass of champagne to the health of the bride and bridegroom." -- Which we did. [Tins END.]</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-02-08_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>EARLESTOWN &amp; NEWTON.</head>
        <p>EARLESTOWN &amp; NEWTON.</p>
        <p>P.S.A.</p>
        <p>The nsusl weekly meeting of the Earlestown and District P.S.A. Society was held in the Co-operative Hall on Sunday afternoon. There was a large attendance over which Mr J J Leatherbarrow pre- sided An address was given by Mr Gilbey of Ashton and recitations by Mr W Morris. Vocal items were given by Miss Corlett, and Dr Middleton presided at the piano. general</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-02-08_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>RUNCORN URBAN DISTRICT COUNCIL!</head>
        <p>RUNCORN URBAN DISTRICT COUNCIL!</p>
        <p>The annual monthly meeting of tile above Counei was held at the Town Hall on Thursday morning, when there were present—Messrs. William Handley (3l.airman) S Taylor, E Imison, D Bisbrown, M Sweetman, J H Salkeld, T Dean, J Wilson, R H Fosnett, S Walton. E Gatley, II G Kirkham, H J Once, George Hewitt, S Wiswall, J Pritchard, T Jones with F A Lake (law clerk), C Marshall (secre- tary), J Wilding (surveyor), and Dr McDougall (medical officer.)</p>
        <p>HEALTH OF THE TOWN. The Medical Officer's report showed that the dis- trict was practical 4 free from disease. During the past month 23 deaths had been registered, two of which were subjects for coroner's inquests. This was an average of 14 per 1,000 of the estimated population, or exclusive of inquest cases an average of 12 per 1,000. The number of births registered was 40. During the corresponding period of last year 26 deaths and 50 births were registered. Mr T Dean remarked it was very gratifying to know that the death rate had got down to 12 per 1,000 of the estimated population. It was some time since it had been so low.</p>
        <p>The Chairman agreed saying it was indeed a very satisfactory state of things.</p>
        <p>THE COST OF THE COMMON. Mr D Bisbrown asked what was the amount already expended upon the Common. He saw that the Highway and Buildings Committee recom- mended that the Common Sub-committee author- ised to purchase trees and shrubs for the common and considered that a little iniormation would be welcome.</p>
        <p>Mr J Wilding (the surveyor) quoted £1,561 6s 93 as the. amount already .spent.</p>
        <p>31r D Bisbrown : There was a sum voted was there not?</p>
        <p>The Chairmen: Yes, I believe some £2,200. INSURING THE WORKMEN.</p>
        <p>Mr S Taylor moved the adoption of the Highway and Buildings Committee recommendations which provided for a Joint Policy with the Ocean Acci- dent and Guarantee Corporation in respect of the Council's workmen, and whose aggregate wages amounted to £2400 per year. The policy covers, in addition to the Council's legal liability, compensa- tion to any workmen injured in their employ where there is no legal liability, to the extent of half his wages for a period not exceeding 26 weeks,or in the event of a fatal accident to one year's wages not exceeding £lOO. IMPROVEMENT OF THE WESTON WATER SUPPLY. Mr J Pritchard, dealing with the recommenda- tion of the Water Committee, said it was desirable that the water supply to Weston be improved in such a manner as to make it independent of the main running along Weston-road, which also sup- plied Weston Point. Therefore it was recommend- ed by them " that a tank with a capacity of 20,000 gallons and a water tower be erected near the Round House, Weston, in accordance with plan sub- mitted." The water would be forced from the Runcorn Pumping Station into a tank by a ram pump. The estimated cost of the land, tower,tank, mains, enclosing of land, pump and engine, and other works was put down at about £l,lOO, and permis.ion would be asked of the Local Government Board for its borrowing, and as the works were of a permanent nature, it was expected that it could be obtained repayable in 30 years. Mr Grice seconded, and the recommendations were adipted.</p>
        <p>DESIRABLE WATEn</p>
        <p>The Water Committee further recommended that application be made to the Local Government Board to sanction the borrowing of a sum of £BOO for the provision of a travelling crane at the pumping sta- tion and of a new boiler, with consequent altera tions to buildings, the repayment of the loan for the crane and buildings to extend over a period of 30 years and the loan for the boiler over a period of 15 years.</p>
        <p>The Committee also reported that the 3in water main in Canal-st. and along the Canal bank was in- sufficient to supply the works in that neighbour- hood as well as for domestic purposes, and there- fore recommended that application be made to the Local Government Board to sanction the borrowing of a sum of •.£4OO to be repaid within a period not exceeding 30 years for thetpurpose of defraying the cost of substituting a six inch main for the 3 inch one. The distance from Dolph Bridge to the Aus- tralian Alum Co's. works being 1,200 lineal yards. Mr J Pritchard dealing with the above minutes of the Water Committee, said the whole Of the out- lay was in the interests of the works, and would not be necessary if the Council adhered to its domestic supply only. The Alum Company had written to them time after time complaining of the manner in which they were hampered for the want of water, and as they were aware some of the works in the district were very large consumers of water and ran to £2OO or £3OO per annum. When the mains had been widened they would be in a position to meet not only the domestic requirements of that locality, but also the requirements of the works, and they felt that something should be done for the works or perhaps they would not remain amongst them at all. Mr. Grice seconded. The recommendations were unanimously agreed to.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-02-08_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>TRIBUTE TO THE CHESHIRE COUNTY COUNCIL.</head>
        <p>A TRIBUTE TO THE CHESHIRE COUNTY COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>The Technical Instruction Committee having had under consideration a letter from the Cheshire County Council, wherein it was pointed out that their Technical Instruction Committee bad been recognised by the Board of Education under regula- tion VII of the Science end Art Directory, as res- p-meible for Science and Art Instruction within the administrative county, recommended that the Council do join the county organization. Mr Wiswall in proposing this said he felt he was in a position to put it strongly before them. From ' his own experience of the County Council he felt it would be a wise and prudent thing to trust them. They had always been friends to education and always generous to their district. It was therefore only due to them to pay a tribute to the County Council for all they have done. lie was sure if it was not for the help they had had from the County Council, though he did not be-little the support received from other quarters, they would not have had such a noble building as they now had in the town. (Hear, hear,)</p>
        <p>Mr Wilson seconded and the motion was agreed to.</p>
        <p>It was also decided that Miss Douglas, of the Liverpool School of Cookery (Princess-road Centre) be engaged to teach cookery in the Day and Even- ing Classes, in place of Miss Caughey, resigned ; and that the Chairman and Committee, Headmaster and secretary be authorised to engage a Laboratory Assistant.</p>
        <p>THE MIAMIAN'S COMMUNICATIONS</p>
        <p>included a letter from the local Government Board intimating that an inquiry will take place at the Town Hall, Runcorn, on Thursday next, re- specting the Council's application to borrow .£lO.OOO for the purposes of the Widnes and Run- corn Bridge Act. THE CHAIRMA.N'S PHOTO.</p>
        <p>Mr Imison, as chairman of the Finance Corn- mittes, and directly responsible for the decoration of the Council Chamber, took the opportunity of drawing the attentionfof the members to the fact that since their last. gathering the walls had been decorated with an excellent portrait of the chair- man. In the course of a short eulogy of Mr Band- ley' work Mr. Imison said no man had rendered such long and capable services to the ratepayers as had Mr. Handley, and he thought it was a very suitable occasion for perpetuating the memory of so good a public servant, and that the fact should , be record.d on the minutes. Mr Handley suitably acknowledged. The Council then went into committee.</p>
        <p>DELICIOUS MAZAWATTEE TEA DELICIOUS MAZWATTEIC TEA DELICIOUS MAZAWATTEK TEA SOLE AGENTS : HENRY MILLING AND CO.. LTD WHOLESALE AND RETAIL GROCERS ETC.. 5, HORSEMARKET-STREET, WIIMINGToN.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-02-08_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>WORKINGMEN'S MISSION, BANK QUAY.</head>
        <p>WORKINGMEN'S MISSION, BANK QUAY.</p>
        <p>Congregational Tea and Entertainment.</p>
        <p>Despite the unfavourable state of the elements, the annual congregational teaparty and entertain- ment in connection with the above mission, which took place in the commodious schools in Thewlis- street on Monday evening, passed off with gratify- in:slice-Is. No doubt the weather,to some extent, militated against the attendance, but nevertheless upwards of 150 persons sat down to the capital tea which was served at six o'clock. Valuable assist- ance at the tables was rendered by the following ladies .—Mesdames Ashley, T Dale, Edwards, D T Taylor, Burndred, G Dale, J Taylor, R. Tanner and W Unsworth and Miss Walllington Afterltea, an entertainment was held, Mr A H Crosfield presiding. There was a large audience, who showed in a full measure their appreciation of the items which were submitted. The artistes were all heard to the best advantage, and several de- mands for repetition had to be complied with. Miss L Davies possesses a rich soprano voice and she 'delighted her hearers with fine interpretations of "The May morning" and "The swallows." For the latter contribution she was vociferously encored and responded with that tuneful song "The dear little shamrock." Mr Tom Boothroyd's banjo quartette had a cordial reception and acquitted themselves admirably. Their selec- tions were " Cromartie march," 'Starlight waltz," and "Belle of Scarboro' dance." Mr Boothroyd played a couple of violin solos" Valse de Concert," and " The Broken Melody" with a taste and expression which gained the hearty plaudits of the audience. A duet entitled" The Battle Eve " was rendered with spirit by Messrs A Griffiths and A Cotterill, and each of these vocalists gave individual contributions. Mr Griffiths feelingly sang the old favourite " Mary of Argyle," for which he was re- called. In response he gave "Fiona"with equal suc- cess. Mr Cotterill gained high encomiums by the effective manner in which he sang" The Admiral's Broom" and " The Midshipmite." Unstinted praise is due to Mr E Wilkes, who ably accom- panied and also played a pianoforte solo of his own composition. The humorous portion of the programme was in the capable hands of Mr. E A Sanders, who fully maintained his good reputation. Ilis efforts to amuse met with complete success, and he had to respond to an encore for his sketch "The Liverwing Testimonial," which kept the aud- ience in roars of laughter. . _</p>
        <p>During an interval the Secretary (Hr. Edwards) presented the annual report upon the past year's work.</p>
        <p>Mr. CROSFIELD, who was cordially received, said : I must in the first place congratulate your secretary on the very admirable report he has just read, so well expressed and so sincere as it was in every paragraph. Be has been good enough to allude to my father who is at present staying at Folkestone, and lam glad to tell you that although when I Ant went to stay there I did not see the progress one would wish t see, on the second occasion when I visited him them was a marked improvement. I can assure you that the family are all very thankful to seethe dear old pater, if not yet quite his old aelf, over so much better. (Applause.) A few minutes before I came to this meeting I r3ceived a tele- gram from my father, wishing you all possible success, and adding that he and Mrs. Crosfield hoped to be with ycu on the next occasion next year. I am sorry my father is not here to-night, because h 3 has always taken a very great and very sincere interest in the welfare of this institution. (Applause). You hear it said now-a-days often enough that philan- thropy is all humbaig, and that people who do benevolent actions usually have some little axe of;their own to grind. I hope it is not bad taste es one of the family to speak to you on the matter, but I can say this that my father had no axe to grind, and what ha has done for this institution or for anything elsothe has tried to do for the benefit of the old Own and his fellow townspeople. (Applause). It has been done out of the kindness of his bent with the hope of being able to further, if possible, the well-being and progress of this part of the town. (Applause). 1 spiak t you with mixed feelings. lam very prowl t 3 stand where my father has stood before me, but I wish he were here him- self instead. I have complimeated the secretary upon the very appropriate and encouraging report he has read to us. Very naturally he touched in the conclusion of the report on the event which has been present lately in all our minds and has touched our hearts. I, was in London when the news cam. It was as though the people had lost a near and dear rotative. Looking around at the subdued manner and the , grave faces of the people of the great metropolis one called to the mind the noble words of the aid tribune -</p>
        <p>the people, Mr Bright, many years ago, at the time of the Crimean war whoa he said in his beautiful language that it seemed to him that the angel of death had been over the land and they could almost hear the beating of his wings. It also reminded me of the effect upon England not very long since when another great tribune of the people was taken from them, another "light that passed from the revolving year." People say that smneeme roar tees haeraeps ph ho irita, obt uh tr olp tyh bi nokt sinufft he re sfea r m daysor ewfhreonmmt eh ne selfishness of cheap cynicism, we feel every year that passes how much we have lost and how much we miss that great leader of the people whose departure left the greatest gap in the best traditions of our public life. (Applause). Yet even the death of Mr Gladstone did not cause such a far reaching and universal sense of loss as is felt now throughout the whole civilised world in the death of the august lady, who was herself a great leader of her people, a great influence for good and our most cherished link to the wonderful century we have left behind Us. One striking ineideat we should not, I think, pass over in silence, even in our little gathering this evening, an event of deep significance for us all. I mean the pre- sence and the affectionate solicitude of the German Emperor. (Hear,hear.) The way in which at a moment's notice be cancelled the important functions and state celebrations of his own country and hastened to the bed- side oi our Queen—all that he has done at this time has touched the heart and aroused the warm gratitude of England and will not be forgotten. (Applause.) No international commercial rivalry can affect, or does affect, in the slightest degree the deep respect and admiration we feel for this grea: ruler, whose influence in the Councils and on the destinies of the world in- creases year by year, and is duo not alone to his lofty station but to the marked ability and capacity of a really great man. (Applause.) The friendship between him and our king is a political fact of the first impor- tance.aCompetition both nations have to face,and the Germans just as much or more than ourselves must reckon with nature's stern and pitiless law, the survival, of the fittest and the consequent struggle for existence instanceanother the best guarantee against the supreme horrors of a European conflagration. (Hear, hear.) T tEtwu ot c opneoooprl de s a nadr ea mai nt y imn mt neen speo i tbioc boon al rett ao t i obnost he f talae an Tins national d noble life. Some years ago I stopped for a week-end at Avignon among those classic and historic surroundings kbee troe a Toro tnot. ah a Isa srt leocaa l edo memorial tl h etro Inawtio where John Stuart Mill spent the last 17 years of his life. I went to visit the modest little home, where as Mr John Morley so beautifully describes it " Mill during these last years of his life kept alight the pale flame of strenu- ous endeavour." After I had seen the unpretending homestead I went as many English travellers have done to see the beautiful memorial Mill raised to his wifeeend I think the epitaph is the noblest tribute to lofty womanhood that ever was penned by man. lie tees ue that " her great and generous heart, her noble soul, her clear, pewerful and clinprehensive intellect were alike the guide and support of those who had the happiness to belong to her." I wish I could give you word for word this exquisite and touching eulogy. I remember Mills' reference to her activity in all works for the public good and at the same time her ready sympathy and kindness in private life. Then after speaking of the irreparable loss he had sustained in her death, he concluded " Were there even but a few like her this earth mi- ht become the hoped for heaven." Well at this mement we remember our old Qaeen's active and deep interest in all that made for the welfare and progress of her people. Of her too We may say—and it is difficult to pay a higher tribute— that to her latest breath she kept alight the pale flame of strenuous endeavour. (Applause). With regard to the King we all know that his real good-heartedness, tact and bonhomme will stand him in good stead, and his great common sense and tactful judgment will make him very popular in good old Merrfe England. (Applause). D) any of tes, I wonder, envy him the burden of responsibility that has devolved upon him during these eventful days, and in truth, ladies and gentlemen, they have been eventful, the like of which none of us here is destined to whams again. It is but a short time since we passed the threshold of a new century, an impressive moment even for the most thoughtless of us; and now has come an event which more than any thing else perhaps as a nation must give us pause. (Hear, hear.) We feel we are at the parting of the ways. The old order ehangeth and givoth place to the new. We are as it were, at the end of a long reach in the great stream of time. As We tound the head we look back on that mighty stretch of `4vaters wiee all its multifarious fleets and argosies. Oa either bank ietiat scenes pass before our minds eye ; the hum and smoke ot commerce, the pemp and circum- stance, the heroism and horror too of war, the enduring triumphs of peace, the fairy land of science—that science which is or may be, a fairy godmother of mankind.</p>
        <p>DELICIOUS MAZAWATTEE TEA DELICIOUS MAZAWATTEE TEA DELICIOUS MAZA VAT IliZ TEA SOLEAGENTS: HENRY MILLING AND CO.. LTD. WHOLESALE AND RETAIL GROCERS ETC., 5, HORSEMAIIKET-STREET, WARRINGT9I.I.</p>
        <p>And looking forward! Why we must feel ourselves as, Newton put it in his great simile:like children picking up shells on the sea-shore, whale the ocean of truth lies un- explored beyond. Whatever may be the destiny of the nation, we shall feel each of us in carryinz out our own tiny share of the/ work, that the life:of our Queen has. been an inspiration to the youngest and an example to the oldest of her subjects in the land. (Applause.) Shortly before the close of the proceedin as, Mr Ashley proposed a vote of thanks to Mr Crosficld for his kindness in occupying the chair. He said they deeply regretted that his father was unable uo be present because for the last thirty years, hir. John Crosfield had invariably attended their annual gatherings. Whilst they regretted his absence they rejoiced that hi g son had seen fit to take his place so well. (Applause.) He (Mr Ashley) was convinced that the district of Bank Quay ought to be proud that there was such a man as Mr John ens- field. (Applause.) They felt deeply indebted to th 3 Crosfield family for what they had done on behalf of that institution. They had found the tools in the shape of money and it was for them (the audience) to work the. institution of which they had heard the annual report; Mr DALE seconded and the motion was carried with, acclamation.</p>
        <p>Mr CROSFIELD briefly aeknewleiged the complimeate.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-02-08_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>WAR.</head>
        <p>THE WAR.</p>
        <p>LATEST TELEGRAMS.</p>
        <p>The Invasion of Cape Colony.</p>
        <p>Sharp Fighting.</p>
        <p>Gordon Highlanders severely Handled.</p>
        <p>CAPETOWN, WEDNESDAY,</p>
        <p>Colonel Da Lido' olumn left Claawilliam on the 29th of January, and after an arduous march through exceedingly difficult country arrivad at Elandsvlei, on the Dooen River, no the eveniaz of the 2od February.</p>
        <p>In a skirmish with the Boers on the 30th January a small patrol of Gordan Highlanders suffered severely. Captain Goalen being wounded, and Lieu- tenant Cowes, and one man killed. Th9re 973.8 no further fighting.</p>
        <p>Yeomanry in Action. An Officer and Some Men Missing. CAPETOWN, Wednesday. A patrol of Imperial Yeomanry came into e ontaet with the invaders at Prince Albert. An officer and some men are missing.</p>
      </div>
